const RAW_AP_ENV_SCIENCE = `
Unit 1 – The Living World: Ecosystems

1.1 Introduction to Ecosystems and Biomes (Terrestrial and Aquatic)

An ecosystem is a community of living organisms (biotic factors) interacting with each other and with their nonliving environment (abiotic factors) such as temperature, water, sunlight, and soil. Ecosystems can range in scale from a rotting log to an entire ocean basin, but all share the same fundamental structure: producers capturing energy, consumers transferring it, decomposers recycling nutrients, and abiotic conditions shaping which organisms can survive there. The interactions among biotic and abiotic components determine the ecosystem's structure, function, and resilience.

Biomes are large-scale ecosystems characterized by dominant vegetation, climate, and associated animal communities. Terrestrial biomes include tropical rainforests (high rainfall and temperature, high biodiversity), temperate deciduous forests (seasonal temperature variation, moderate rainfall), grasslands and savannas (low rainfall, dominated by grasses), deserts (very low rainfall, extreme temperature swings), taiga or boreal forests (long cold winters, coniferous trees), and tundra (extremely cold, permafrost, very low productivity). Each biome is defined primarily by its climate, particularly temperature and precipitation patterns, which determine the dominant plant forms.

Aquatic biomes cover the majority of Earth's surface and are broadly divided into freshwater (rivers, lakes, wetlands, ponds) and marine (oceans, estuaries, coral reefs, intertidal zones) systems. Aquatic biomes are characterized by factors including salinity, water temperature, depth, light penetration, and nutrient availability. Estuaries, where freshwater meets saltwater, are among the most productive ecosystems on Earth because of the high nutrient input from rivers. Coral reefs, though covering a small area, support extraordinary biodiversity and are highly sensitive to temperature and pH changes. Understanding biome characteristics is foundational to predicting how ecosystems respond to environmental disturbances.

Key ideas: Ecosystems consist of biotic and abiotic components in constant interaction. Terrestrial biomes are defined by climate (temperature and precipitation). Aquatic biomes are defined by salinity, depth, light, and nutrients. Estuaries and coral reefs are among the most productive and biodiverse aquatic ecosystems.

1.2 Biogeochemical Cycles (Carbon, Nitrogen, Phosphorus, Hydrologic)

Biogeochemical cycles describe the movement of chemical elements and compounds through the biotic (living) and abiotic (nonliving) components of an ecosystem. Unlike energy, which flows in one direction and is lost as heat, matter is continuously recycled through these cycles. The carbon cycle involves photosynthesis (removing CO2 from the atmosphere), cellular respiration (returning CO2), decomposition, combustion of fossil fuels (rapidly releasing stored carbon), and ocean absorption. Human burning of fossil fuels has significantly accelerated the addition of carbon to the atmosphere, disrupting the balance of the natural carbon cycle.

The nitrogen cycle is critical because nitrogen is an essential component of proteins and nucleic acids, yet most organisms cannot use atmospheric nitrogen (N2) directly. Nitrogen fixation by specialized bacteria (such as Rhizobium in legume root nodules and free-living cyanobacteria) converts N2 into ammonium (NH4+), which plants can absorb. Nitrification converts ammonium to nitrite and then nitrate (NO3-) by nitrifying bacteria. Plants take up nitrate; animals obtain nitrogen by consuming plants or other animals. Decomposers break down organic nitrogen back into ammonium (ammonification), and denitrifying bacteria convert nitrates back to N2, completing the cycle. Human applications of nitrogen fertilizers have greatly increased reactive nitrogen in ecosystems, causing eutrophication and other problems.

The phosphorus cycle lacks a significant atmospheric component, cycling primarily through rock weathering, soil, water, and living organisms. Phosphorus is a limiting nutrient in many aquatic ecosystems, and its release from agricultural runoff frequently triggers algal blooms. The hydrologic cycle describes the movement of water through evaporation from oceans and land surfaces, transpiration from plants, condensation into clouds, precipitation, surface runoff, infiltration into groundwater, and return to the ocean via rivers. Human activities including deforestation, urbanization, and groundwater extraction alter the hydrologic cycle by reducing infiltration, increasing runoff, and changing evapotranspiration rates.

Key ideas: Matter is recycled through biogeochemical cycles unlike energy which flows unidirectionally. Carbon cycle is disrupted by fossil fuel combustion. Nitrogen requires biological fixation before most organisms can use it. Phosphorus cycles through rocks and water without an atmospheric phase and is a common limiting nutrient. Human activities alter all four cycles.

1.3 Primary Productivity and Trophic Levels

Primary productivity is the rate at which producers (plants, algae, and photosynthetic bacteria) convert solar energy into chemical energy through photosynthesis. Gross primary productivity (GPP) is the total amount of energy fixed by photosynthesis; net primary productivity (NPP) is GPP minus the energy used by the producers themselves for cellular respiration. NPP represents the energy actually available to consumers in the ecosystem and is the basis of all ecosystem food webs. NPP varies dramatically across ecosystems: tropical rainforests and estuaries have very high NPP; deserts and open ocean have very low NPP.

Trophic levels organize organisms based on their feeding position in the food chain. Producers (autotrophs) occupy the first trophic level. Primary consumers (herbivores) eat producers and occupy the second trophic level. Secondary consumers eat primary consumers; tertiary consumers eat secondary consumers. Decomposers (bacteria and fungi) break down dead organic matter at all trophic levels and are essential for nutrient recycling. Some organisms occupy multiple trophic levels as omnivores, and many ecosystems have complex feeding relationships that do not fit neatly into a linear chain.

Trophic level concepts are directly connected to energy transfer efficiency and are foundational to understanding food webs, biomass pyramids, and the ecological impact of top predator removal. AP Environmental Science frequently asks students to calculate NPP, compare productivity across biomes, explain why higher trophic levels support less biomass, and connect primary productivity to ecosystem health. Limiting factors for primary productivity include light, water, temperature, and nutrient availability, particularly nitrogen and phosphorus.

Key ideas: GPP is total photosynthesis; NPP = GPP minus producer respiration. NPP determines energy available to consumers. Trophic levels organize feeding relationships from producers through successive consumers. Limiting nutrients (nitrogen, phosphorus) and light most commonly constrain primary productivity.

1.4 Energy Flow, 10% Rule, Food Chains, and Food Webs

Energy flows through ecosystems in one direction, from the sun through producers to successive consumers, and is lost as heat at every transformation. The 10% rule states that on average only about 10% of the energy at one trophic level is available to the next, because the other 90% is lost through cellular respiration, heat generation, waste, and material not consumed. This means a food chain starting with 10,000 kilocalories of plant energy can support approximately 1,000 kilocalories of herbivore biomass, 100 kilocalories of primary carnivore biomass, and only 10 kilocalories of secondary carnivore biomass. This exponential decrease limits food chain length and explains why pyramids of energy and biomass narrow dramatically from base to apex.

Food chains represent linear feeding sequences (grass is eaten by mice, which are eaten by snakes, which are eaten by hawks). Food webs are more realistic representations of feeding relationships in ecosystems, showing the interconnected network of multiple food chains and the complex dependencies among species. Food webs demonstrate that the removal of a single species can have cascading effects throughout the entire network, particularly when a keystone species is involved. Trophic cascades occur when changes in top predator populations ripple down through the food web, affecting herbivore populations and vegetation simultaneously.

The 10% rule has direct practical implications for food production and sustainability. Eating lower on the food chain (consuming plants rather than animals) is far more efficient in terms of land use, water, and energy because fewer trophic transfers occur. For example, producing one kilogram of beef requires approximately 10 kilograms of grain, reflecting the energy losses at each trophic level. These efficiency considerations are central to discussions of sustainable agriculture, meat production, and global food security on the AP exam.

Key ideas: Energy flows one way through ecosystems and is lost as heat at each trophic transfer. 10% rule: only about 10% of energy transfers between trophic levels. Food webs show interconnected feeding relationships; removing one species can cascade through the web. Lower trophic feeding is more energy efficient, with implications for sustainable food production.

1.5 Ecosystem Structure and Interactions

Ecosystems are structured by the types and densities of species they contain and by the interactions among those species. Competition occurs when two species (or individuals within a species) compete for the same limiting resource, resulting in harm to both. Predation benefits the predator while harming the prey; over time, coevolution shapes both predator efficiency and prey defenses. Mutualism benefits both species (nitrogen-fixing bacteria and legumes; mycorrhizal fungi and plant roots). Commensalism benefits one species without affecting the other. Parasitism harms the host while benefiting the parasite. These interactions together determine the relative abundance of species and the overall structure of the community.

Keystone species have effects on ecosystem structure disproportionate to their abundance. Sea otters controlling sea urchin populations (which would otherwise overgraze kelp forests), wolves regulating elk behavior and allowing riparian vegetation to recover in Yellowstone, and fig trees providing year-round fruit in tropical forests are classic examples. The removal of a keystone species destabilizes the ecosystem in ways far exceeding what would be predicted from its biomass or number alone. Identifying keystone species and understanding the consequences of their removal is a key AP Environmental Science concept.

Ecosystem services are the benefits that functioning ecosystems provide to humans, including provisioning services (food, water, timber, medicines), regulating services (climate regulation, flood control, air purification, water filtration), cultural services (recreation, spiritual value, aesthetic enjoyment), and supporting services (nutrient cycling, soil formation, primary productivity). Quantifying the economic value of ecosystem services has become an important tool for conservation policy, demonstrating that protecting natural ecosystems is often far more economically rational than destroying them for short-term resource extraction.

Key ideas: Species interactions (competition, predation, mutualism, commensalism, parasitism) determine community structure. Keystone species have outsized ecological effects; their removal destabilizes ecosystems. Ecosystem services include provisioning, regulating, cultural, and supporting services. Recognizing ecosystem service value is central to conservation policy arguments.


Unit 2 – The Living World: Biodiversity

2.1 Introduction to Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services

Biodiversity refers to the variety of life on Earth and is typically measured at three levels: genetic diversity (variation in DNA within a species), species diversity (the number and relative abundance of species in a community), and ecosystem diversity (the variety of habitat types and ecological communities across a region). High biodiversity generally correlates with greater ecosystem stability, productivity, and resilience, because diverse communities have more functional redundancy (multiple species performing similar ecological roles) and more complex interaction networks that can buffer disturbances.

Species richness is the simple count of species in an area; species evenness refers to the relative abundance of those species. Two communities can have identical species richness but very different diversity if one is dominated by a single species while the other has equal proportions of each. Biodiversity hotspots are regions with exceptionally high species richness and endemism (species found nowhere else) that are under significant threat from human activities. Examples include the tropical Andes, the Mediterranean Basin, the Cape Floristic Region of South Africa, and the islands of the Philippines and Caribbean.

The relationship between biodiversity and ecosystem services is well established: more diverse ecosystems provide more reliable and varied services. Forests with diverse tree species are more resistant to pest outbreaks than monocultures; diverse coral reef communities support more productive fisheries; genetically diverse crop wild relatives provide resistance genes essential for developing pest and disease resistant cultivars. The loss of biodiversity therefore threatens not only ecological function but also human well-being and economic productivity in direct and measurable ways.

Key ideas: Biodiversity includes genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity. Species richness and evenness together measure community diversity. Biodiversity hotspots have high endemism and face significant threats. High biodiversity increases ecosystem stability and reliability of ecosystem services.

2.2 Island Biogeography and Ecological Tolerance

Island biogeography theory, developed by MacArthur and Wilson, predicts the number of species on an island based on two opposing processes: immigration (colonization of new species from the mainland) and extinction (local loss of species already established). Larger islands support more species because they provide more habitat and resources, reducing extinction rates. Islands closer to the mainland source pool support more species because immigration rates are higher. The equilibrium species number is reached when immigration and extinction rates are equal, and this equilibrium is dynamic (species are continually turning over even at a constant richness level).

Island biogeography has important applications beyond literal islands. Habitat fragments created by deforestation, urbanization, or agriculture function as ecological islands, with the same dynamics governing species persistence. Larger habitat patches retain more species; isolated patches lose species more rapidly to extinction. These principles directly inform reserve design in conservation biology: larger, rounder (less edge), and well-connected reserves support more biodiversity than small, fragmented, isolated ones. Wildlife corridors connecting habitat fragments facilitate immigration and reduce extinction rates, applying island biogeography principles in practical conservation settings.

Ecological tolerance describes the range of abiotic conditions within which a species can survive and reproduce. The fundamental niche is the full range of conditions a species can theoretically occupy; the realized niche is the narrower subset actually occupied due to competition, predation, and other biotic factors. Liebig's law of the minimum states that the single most limiting resource constrains a species regardless of the abundance of other resources. Tolerance curves show the optimal range and tolerance limits for individual environmental factors; species with wide tolerances (eurythermal, for example) are generalists, while species with narrow tolerances are specialists more vulnerable to environmental change.

Key ideas: Island biogeography: more species on larger, closer islands where immigration exceeds extinction. Habitat fragments function as ecological islands; conservation reserves benefit from being large, connected, and minimally fragmented. Ecological tolerance defines where species can survive; limiting factors constrain distributions regardless of other resource abundances.

2.3 Natural Disruptions and Adaptations

Natural disruptions, including wildfires, floods, droughts, hurricanes, volcanic eruptions, and disease outbreaks, are integral parts of ecosystem dynamics rather than aberrations. Many ecosystems are adapted to, and in some cases dependent on, natural disturbances. Fire-adapted ecosystems such as chaparral and longleaf pine forests require periodic burning to maintain their structure: fire-resistant plants resprout rapidly, fire-adapted seeds germinate in response to heat or smoke, and the removal of accumulated fuel and competing vegetation creates habitat conditions these communities require. Fire suppression in such ecosystems leads to fuel accumulation and ultimately more destructive fires.

Species and ecosystems have evolved diverse adaptations in response to natural disturbances. Serotinous pine cones (which only open and release seeds after exposure to fire heat), flood-plain species with specialized root systems that can survive inundation, drought-tolerant succulents with water storage tissues, and migratory species that track resource availability across landscapes are all examples of evolutionary responses to natural disruption. The intermediate disturbance hypothesis proposes that species diversity is maximized at intermediate levels of disturbance: too little disturbance allows competitive exclusion by dominant species; too much prevents most species from establishing; intermediate disturbance maintains a mosaic of successional stages supporting high diversity.

Human activities alter natural disturbance regimes in ways that stress ecosystems. Fire suppression in fire-adapted systems, stream channelization that eliminates natural flood dynamics, overgrazing that alters vegetation structure, and climate change that increases disturbance frequency and intensity all disrupt the disturbance regimes to which ecosystems are adapted. Understanding natural disturbance ecology is essential for managing ecosystems sustainably, because management that prevents natural processes often degrades the very ecosystems it intends to protect.

Key ideas: Natural disturbances (fire, flood, drought) are integral parts of ecosystem dynamics, not simply damage. Many ecosystems are adapted to or dependent on disturbance. The intermediate disturbance hypothesis: diversity is maximized at intermediate disturbance levels. Human disruption of natural disturbance regimes (fire suppression, channelization) often degrades ecosystem health.

2.4 Ecological Succession and Ecosystem Recovery

Ecological succession is the directional, predictable change in community composition and structure over time following a disturbance or in a newly available habitat. Primary succession occurs on previously lifeless substrate with no soil, such as bare rock exposed by a retreating glacier or newly solidified lava. Pioneer species, typically lichens and mosses, colonize first, tolerate harsh conditions, and begin to break down rock and add organic matter, gradually creating soil that allows later successional species to establish. Primary succession is slow because soil development is the rate-limiting process.

Secondary succession occurs in areas where a community was disrupted but soil and seed banks remain, as after a fire, flood, agricultural abandonment, or logging. Secondary succession proceeds much faster than primary succession because the substrate already contains organic matter, seeds, spores, and root systems from the previous community. Old field succession in the eastern United States, for example, transitions from annual grasses and forbs to perennial grasses, shrubs, early successional trees (pine, birch), and eventually climax deciduous forest over decades to centuries. Each successional stage modifies the environment in ways that favor the establishment of the next stage (facilitation) or simply provides the time necessary for slower-growing species to establish (tolerance).

The climax community concept describes a relatively stable endpoint of succession, in equilibrium with the regional climate. In reality, few ecosystems reach or maintain a static climax state; disturbances continually reset patches of the landscape to earlier successional stages, creating a shifting mosaic of successional ages that supports higher overall diversity than any single stage alone would. Understanding succession is practically important for ecosystem restoration, as managers can accelerate recovery by reintroducing appropriate species, controlling invasives, and restoring soil and hydrology to enable the successional process to proceed.

Key ideas: Primary succession begins on bare substrate; pioneer species initiate soil development. Secondary succession is faster because soil and seed banks remain. Each successional stage modifies conditions for the next. Climax communities are dynamic, maintained by a shifting disturbance mosaic. Succession principles guide ecosystem restoration practice.

2.5 Biodiversity Conservation and Importance

Biodiversity is threatened by habitat destruction and fragmentation (the leading cause of species loss globally), overexploitation (hunting, fishing, and harvesting beyond sustainable rates), invasive species (outcompeting or preying on native species without natural controls), pollution (contaminating habitats and directly harming species), and climate change (shifting habitat conditions faster than many species can adapt or migrate). These threats often interact and amplify each other: a species already stressed by habitat loss is less able to cope with the additional pressure of climate-induced range shifts, creating extinction debts (species committed to eventual extinction even if threats are reduced).

Conservation biology approaches biodiversity protection at multiple scales. Species-level conservation includes captive breeding programs, legal protections under the Endangered Species Act, and reintroduction programs. Habitat-level conservation focuses on protecting, managing, and restoring the ecosystems on which species depend, including the designation of protected areas, restoration of degraded habitats, and management of the surrounding landscape matrix. Landscape-level conservation addresses connectivity among habitat patches, wildlife corridors, and the management of entire watersheds and regions. International agreements including CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) and the Convention on Biological Diversity coordinate global conservation efforts.

The intrinsic value of biodiversity (the value of species regardless of their usefulness to humans) and the instrumental value (what biodiversity does for humans through ecosystem services, medicines, food security, and cultural benefits) are both important frameworks for conservation policy. Economic arguments for biodiversity conservation include the value of genetic resources for agriculture and medicine, the direct economic value of tourism to biodiverse regions, and the replacement costs of ecosystem services (water purification, flood control, carbon sequestration) that would need to be provided artificially if natural systems are destroyed. AP Environmental Science expects students to evaluate conservation strategies, explain the importance of biodiversity, and connect species loss to broader ecological and human welfare consequences.

Key ideas: Biodiversity is threatened by habitat loss, overexploitation, invasive species, pollution, and climate change, often acting simultaneously. Conservation operates at species, habitat, and landscape scales. Both intrinsic and instrumental values of biodiversity support conservation. Legal and international frameworks (ESA, CITES, Convention on Biological Diversity) coordinate protection efforts.


Unit 3 – Populations

3.1 Generalist vs. Specialist and r/K Selected Species

Generalist species can tolerate a wide range of environmental conditions and use many different resources, allowing them to persist in disturbed or variable habitats. White-tailed deer, raccoons, crows, rats, and many weedy plants are generalists that thrive alongside human development because they exploit diverse food sources and tolerate habitat modification. Specialist species have narrow ecological requirements (specific food types, precise habitat conditions, particular temperature ranges) and are more vulnerable to environmental change because they cannot compensate by shifting to alternative resources or habitats. Giant pandas dependent on bamboo, koalas dependent on eucalyptus, and many orchid species with single pollinator relationships are classic specialists.

The r/K selection continuum describes reproductive strategies that evolution has shaped in response to different ecological pressures. r-selected species (high r, the intrinsic rate of increase) invest in producing many small offspring with little parental care, reach reproductive maturity quickly, have short lifespans, and are adapted to unstable, unpredictable, or low-density environments where rapid reproduction is advantageous before conditions deteriorate. Examples include bacteria, annual weeds, insects, and small rodents. K-selected species invest heavily in few offspring with extensive parental care, mature slowly, live long, and are adapted to stable environments at or near carrying capacity (K) where competitive ability and offspring quality matter more than reproductive rate.

In practice, most species fall somewhere along the continuum rather than at the extremes. r and K selection are most useful as a conceptual framework for understanding why different species respond differently to disturbance and exploitation. r-selected species can recover quickly from population declines; K-selected species (elephants, whales, condors, ancient forests) are far more vulnerable to overharvesting and slow to recover because their reproductive rates are low and survival of each individual offspring requires substantial parental investment.

Key ideas: Generalists tolerate wide conditions and thrive in disturbed habitats; specialists have narrow requirements and are more extinction-vulnerable. r-selected species produce many small offspring rapidly; K-selected produce few large offspring with high parental investment. r-selected species recover faster from population reductions; K-selected are more vulnerable to overexploitation.

3.2 Survivorship Curves and Carrying Capacity

Survivorship curves graphically represent the proportion of individuals in a cohort (a group born at the same time) that survive to each age. Type I survivorship curves show high survival throughout most of life with mortality concentrated at old age, typical of large K-selected animals with significant parental investment (humans, elephants, whales). Type II survivorship curves show a roughly constant mortality rate throughout life, typical of many bird species and some reptiles. Type III survivorship curves show very high mortality of young individuals with those few who survive early life having good prospects for reaching maturity, typical of r-selected species producing many small offspring (oysters, most fish, many insects, oaks producing thousands of acorns).

Carrying capacity (K) is the maximum population size that a given environment can sustainably support, limited by available resources including food, water, shelter, and territory. Populations growing toward carrying capacity display logistic growth (S-shaped or sigmoidal curve): rapid exponential growth when population density is low and resources are abundant, then slowing growth as density increases and resources become limiting, approaching a stable maximum near K. Density-dependent factors (competition for resources, disease transmission, predation pressure) intensify as population density increases, providing negative feedback that drives the population toward K.

Overshoot occurs when a population temporarily exceeds its carrying capacity, typically followed by a population crash as resource depletion reduces K below the population size. Humans have temporarily increased Earth's apparent carrying capacity through agricultural technology, fossil fuel use, and resource extraction, but these interventions do not necessarily increase the long-term sustainable carrying capacity and may degrade the resource base in ways that reduce K for future generations. Understanding carrying capacity is central to evaluating sustainable resource use and the long-term trajectory of the human population.

Key ideas: Type I survivorship: low early mortality, high late mortality (large K-selected). Type II: constant mortality. Type III: high early mortality, low late mortality (r-selected). Carrying capacity K limits population size through density-dependent negative feedback. Overshoot and crash occur when populations temporarily exceed K. Human technology has increased apparent K but may reduce long-term sustainable K.

3.3 Population Growth and Resource Availability

Population growth is determined by the balance of birth rate, death rate, immigration, and emigration: population change = births plus immigration minus deaths minus emigration. The intrinsic rate of natural increase (r) represents the per capita growth rate under optimal conditions (no resource limitation). Exponential growth (J-shaped curve) occurs when r is constant and positive, with population increasing by a fixed percentage per time period: the larger the population, the faster the absolute increase even when the percentage rate is constant. Exponential growth is unsustainable in the real world because resources are finite.

Resource availability is the primary constraint on population growth in nature. Liebig's law of the minimum states that population size is controlled by the single most limiting resource, regardless of the availability of others. In terrestrial ecosystems, limiting resources commonly include food, water, nesting sites, and territory. In aquatic ecosystems, nutrients (particularly nitrogen and phosphorus) most often limit productivity. When limiting resources are abundant, populations grow toward their biotic potential; as resources are depleted, birth rates fall, death rates rise, and growth slows.

Human populations have historically overcome natural resource limits through technology, trade, and the exploitation of nonrenewable resources. The Green Revolution increased agricultural productivity dramatically, supporting population growth that would not have been possible otherwise; fossil fuels provide energy that dramatically multiplies human productive capacity. However, these resource substitutions have introduced new limiting factors and environmental costs, including soil degradation, groundwater depletion, climate change, and pollution. Environmental science frames the question of human population carrying capacity in terms of these resource and environmental constraints.

Key ideas: Population growth = births plus immigration minus deaths minus emigration. Exponential growth is unsustainable because resources are finite. Liebig's law: the single most limiting resource controls population size. Human populations have temporarily overcome natural limits through technology and fossil fuels but face cumulative resource and environmental constraints.

3.4 Age Structure and Fertility Rates

Age structure diagrams (population pyramids) show the distribution of a population among different age classes, typically divided into pre-reproductive (0 to 14 years), reproductive (15 to 44 years), and post-reproductive (45+ years) groups. The shape of the pyramid reveals the population's recent history and future trajectory. A wide base (many young individuals) indicates high birth rates and a rapidly growing population, characteristic of many developing nations. A roughly uniform distribution (similar numbers in each age class) indicates a stable population. A narrow base (fewer young than old) indicates a declining population, increasingly characteristic of many developed nations.

Total fertility rate (TFR) is the average number of children a woman would have over her lifetime given current age-specific fertility rates. Replacement-level fertility (approximately 2.1 children per woman in developed nations, slightly higher where childhood mortality is greater) is the TFR at which a population exactly replaces itself over time. TFRs below replacement produce population decline in the long run; TFRs above replacement produce growth. Global TFR has declined substantially over the past 50 years, from approximately 5 in 1960 to below 2.5 today, though with enormous regional variation.

Population momentum describes the tendency of a young population to continue growing for decades even after TFR falls to replacement level, because the large cohort of young people has not yet entered its peak reproductive years. Conversely, an aging population can continue to decline for decades even if TFR rises, because the smaller young cohorts produce fewer total births. Understanding age structure and population momentum is essential for projecting future population growth, planning for educational and healthcare needs, and understanding the long-term consequences of current fertility trends.

Key ideas: Age structure diagrams reveal growth trends: wide base means rapid growth, narrow base means decline. TFR of approximately 2.1 is replacement level; below replacement means eventual decline. Population momentum causes continued growth or decline for decades after TFR changes. Age structure analysis predicts future population change and informs social planning.

3.5 Human Population Dynamics and Demographic Transition

The demographic transition model describes how human populations typically progress through four stages as economic development occurs. Stage 1 (pre-industrial) has high birth rates and high death rates, producing low and relatively stable population with low life expectancy. Stage 2 (early industrial) has rapidly declining death rates (due to improved nutrition, sanitation, and medicine) while birth rates remain high, producing rapid population growth. Stage 3 (late industrial) has birth rates declining as urbanization, education, and economic change alter family structure and reduce the economic value of large families. Stage 4 (post-industrial) has both low birth rates and low death rates, producing low or stable population growth. Some demographers add a Stage 5 (sub-replacement fertility) characteristic of highly developed nations where TFR falls below replacement.

The global population reached 8 billion in 2022 and is projected to peak somewhere between 9 and 11 billion in the late 21st century. Nearly all projected future growth will occur in less developed nations, particularly sub-Saharan Africa, while populations in many developed nations are stabilizing or declining. The uneven distribution of population growth creates different challenges: rapidly growing populations stress educational systems, infrastructure, and natural resources; aging, shrinking populations face labor shortages and challenges funding social security and healthcare for large elderly cohorts.

Factors that historically lower fertility rates most effectively include education and economic empowerment of women, access to family planning services, reduced child mortality (reducing the incentive to have many children as insurance against losing some), urbanization (which reduces the economic utility of children as farm labor), and increased old-age security (reducing reliance on children for support in old age). Understanding these drivers connects demographic trends to broader development, gender equity, and public health policy in ways AP Environmental Science frequently examines.

Key ideas: Demographic transition model: Stage 1 (high birth, high death), Stage 2 (death rate drops, rapid growth), Stage 3 (birth rate drops), Stage 4 (low birth, low death, stable population). Global population projected to peak at 9 to 11 billion. Women's education, family planning access, and child survival most effectively reduce fertility. Future growth concentrated in less developed nations.


Unit 4 – Earth Systems and Resources

4.1 Tectonic Plates and Soil Formation

Earth's lithosphere is divided into approximately 15 major tectonic plates that move slowly (a few centimeters per year) on the underlying asthenosphere. Plate boundaries are zones of intense geological activity: convergent boundaries (where plates collide) produce mountain ranges, volcanic arcs, and subduction zones; divergent boundaries (where plates separate) produce mid-ocean ridges and rift valleys; transform boundaries (where plates slide horizontally past each other) produce fault zones and earthquakes. Volcanic activity associated with plate boundaries releases minerals to the surface that eventually weather into soil, connecting tectonic processes to soil formation and terrestrial productivity.

Soil formation (pedogenesis) is a slow process driven by the weathering of parent material (underlying bedrock or sediment), the accumulation of organic matter from decomposing organisms, the activity of soil organisms (bacteria, fungi, earthworms, and other invertebrates), and the influence of climate (temperature and moisture) over time. Physical weathering (freeze-thaw cycles, abrasion by water and wind, root pressure) breaks parent material into smaller particles. Chemical weathering (dissolution, oxidation, hydrolysis) alters mineral composition and releases plant nutrients. The addition of organic matter (humus) from decomposing plants, animals, and microorganisms is critical for soil fertility, water holding capacity, and structure.

The time required for significant soil formation is measured in centuries to millennia. Mature, productive soils represent irreplaceable resources that accumulate over geological time, making soil conservation critically important. Volcanic regions (like Iceland and Hawaii) demonstrate the entire succession from bare rock to developed soil when young lava flows are colonized by pioneer species and undergo pedogenesis over centuries. Understanding the geological and biological processes that create soil provides essential context for evaluating the severity of soil degradation and the urgency of soil conservation.

Key ideas: Tectonic plate movement creates mountain ranges, volcanoes, and earthquakes at boundaries. Soil forms through weathering of parent material, accumulation of organic matter, and biological activity over centuries to millennia. Volcanic minerals enrich soils; soil formation is far slower than soil loss, making conservation critical.

4.2 Soil Composition, Properties, and Erosion

Soil is composed of mineral particles (derived from weathered rock), organic matter (humus), water, air, and living organisms. Mineral particles are classified by size: clay (smallest, less than 0.002 mm), silt (0.002 to 0.05 mm), and sand (0.05 to 2 mm). Loam, a mixture of roughly equal proportions of sand, silt, and clay with significant organic matter, is considered ideal agricultural soil because it balances water retention (clay), drainage (sand), and nutrient availability (organic matter and clay particles with large surface area and negative charges that attract positively charged nutrient ions).

A soil profile consists of distinct horizontal layers called horizons. The O horizon (organic layer) consists of decomposing leaf litter and organic material. The A horizon (topsoil) is rich in organic matter and biological activity, dark in color, and the most fertile layer. The B horizon (subsoil) accumulates minerals leached from above, has less organic matter, and is lighter in color. The C horizon consists of weathered parent material with little biological activity. Below lies R horizon bedrock. Topsoil depth is the most important determinant of agricultural productivity; erosion of topsoil removes the most fertile, biologically active layer.

Soil erosion by water and wind removes topsoil at rates far exceeding natural soil formation, representing one of the most serious global environmental problems. Deforestation, overgrazing, unsustainable tilling, and leaving soil bare between crops all accelerate erosion. Conservation practices including contour plowing (plowing across slopes rather than up and down to reduce water runoff velocity), terracing (building level steps on hillsides), no-till or reduced-till agriculture, cover crops (planted between cash crop seasons to hold soil), windbreaks (rows of trees reducing wind velocity), and riparian buffers (vegetated strips along waterways) all reduce erosion rates. Soil salinization (accumulation of salts from irrigation water evaporation) is another major form of soil degradation affecting dryland agricultural regions.

Key ideas: Ideal agricultural soil (loam) balances sand, silt, clay, and organic matter. Soil horizons: O (organic), A (topsoil, most fertile), B (subsoil), C (parent material). Topsoil erosion removes the most productive layer. Conservation practices (contour plowing, terracing, no-till, cover crops, windbreaks) reduce erosion. Salinization degrades irrigated soils in arid regions.

4.3 Atmosphere and Global Wind Patterns

Earth's atmosphere consists of several layers defined by temperature gradients. The troposphere (0 to approximately 12 km) contains most atmospheric mass, all weather phenomena, and the majority of greenhouse gases; temperature decreases with altitude. The stratosphere (12 to 50 km) contains the ozone layer that absorbs harmful ultraviolet radiation; temperature increases with altitude (due to ozone absorption of UV). The mesosphere and thermosphere lie above. The composition of the troposphere is approximately 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 1% argon, and variable trace gases including carbon dioxide, methane, and water vapor.

Global wind patterns are driven by differential heating of Earth's surface (tropical regions receive more direct solar radiation than polar regions) and the Coriolis effect (the deflection of moving air caused by Earth's rotation). Warm air rises near the equator, creating a low-pressure zone, and colder air sinks near 30 degrees latitude (creating subtropical high-pressure zones, the site of most of Earth's major deserts) and near the poles. This creates three convection cells in each hemisphere: the Hadley cell (0 to 30 degrees), Ferrel cell (30 to 60 degrees), and polar cell (60 to 90 degrees). Surface winds from these cells, deflected by the Coriolis effect, produce the trade winds (blowing northeast to southwest toward the equator in the northern hemisphere), westerlies (blowing southwest to northeast in the mid-latitudes), and polar easterlies.

Global wind and pressure patterns distribute precipitation unevenly: the equatorial low-pressure zone (rising air cools, condenses, heavy rainfall) creates tropical rainforests; the subtropical high-pressure zones (descending dry air) create deserts at approximately 30 degrees latitude; the mid-latitude storm track (convergence of polar and tropical air masses) supports temperate forests and grasslands. Ocean currents, driven by wind stress on the surface and by thermohaline circulation (density differences due to temperature and salinity), redistribute heat globally and moderate coastal climates. Understanding global atmospheric circulation is fundamental to understanding biome distribution, climate patterns, and the impacts of climate change.

Key ideas: Troposphere contains weather and greenhouse gases; stratosphere contains ozone. Global wind patterns result from differential heating and the Coriolis effect, creating three convection cells per hemisphere. Trade winds, westerlies, and polar easterlies are major surface wind belts. Precipitation patterns (tropical rain, subtropical deserts, mid-latitude storms) directly determine biome distribution.

4.4 Watersheds and Water Systems

A watershed (also called a drainage basin or catchment) is the entire land area that drains into a particular river system, lake, or other water body. Every point on land is part of a watershed, and watershed boundaries are defined by topographic ridgelines. Precipitation falling within a watershed either infiltrates into soil (recharging groundwater), evaporates, is transpired by plants (evapotranspiration), or flows as surface runoff into streams and rivers. Watershed health determines downstream water quality and quantity: healthy watersheds with intact vegetation and permeable soils have high infiltration, low peak flows, and clean water; degraded watersheds with bare soil and impervious surfaces have high runoff, flooding, and poor water quality.

Freshwater exists as surface water (rivers, lakes, wetlands) and groundwater (water filling pore spaces in rock and sediment below the water table, stored in aquifers). The vadose zone is the unsaturated soil above the water table where water moves downward toward the saturated zone. Aquifer recharge depends on precipitation and infiltration; aquifer depletion occurs when extraction exceeds recharge. The Ogallala Aquifer underlying the Great Plains of the United States is being depleted far faster than it recharges, representing a serious long-term threat to agricultural water supply in one of the world's most productive farming regions.

Wetlands (marshes, swamps, bogs, fens) are transitional zones between aquatic and terrestrial systems and provide numerous ecosystem services: flood storage and peak flow attenuation, water quality improvement through nutrient uptake and sediment trapping, groundwater recharge, carbon storage (particularly peatlands), and habitat for waterfowl and many other species. Despite their ecological importance, wetlands have historically been drained for agriculture and development. The Clean Water Act in the United States provides some wetland protection through Section 404 permitting requirements for dredging and filling.

Key ideas: Watersheds are the entire land area draining to a water body; vegetation and soil health determine watershed function. Groundwater in aquifers is recharged by infiltration; over-extraction depletes aquifers faster than recharge. Wetlands provide flood attenuation, water quality improvement, carbon storage, and habitat. Wetland loss has been widespread; regulatory protection through the Clean Water Act Section 404 is imperfect.

4.5 Solar Radiation, Climate, and Seasons

Solar radiation drives all of Earth's climate, weather, and biological productivity. The amount of solar energy reaching a given location depends on the angle of incidence (the angle at which sunlight strikes the surface). Near the equator, the sun is nearly overhead at midday year-round, so solar energy strikes the surface at a nearly perpendicular angle, concentrating maximum energy per unit area. At higher latitudes, the sun is lower in the sky and the same amount of energy is spread over a larger area, resulting in less energy per unit area. This latitudinal variation in solar energy input is the fundamental driver of Earth's temperature gradients and global circulation.

Earth's seasons result from the 23.5-degree tilt of Earth's axis relative to its orbit around the sun, not from changes in Earth's distance from the sun (Earth is actually slightly closer to the sun in January than in June). When the Northern Hemisphere is tilted toward the sun (summer solstice, approximately June 21), Northern Hemisphere days are longer and sunlight strikes at a more direct angle, producing warmer temperatures. When the Southern Hemisphere is tilted toward the sun (Northern Hemisphere winter solstice, approximately December 21), Northern Hemisphere days are shorter and sunlight strikes at a more oblique angle, producing colder temperatures. At the equinoxes (approximately March 21 and September 21), neither hemisphere is tilted toward the sun and day length equals night length everywhere on Earth.

The albedo of a surface is the fraction of incoming solar radiation that it reflects. Fresh snow and ice have high albedo (reflecting 80 to 90% of incoming radiation); dark ocean water, forests, and bare soil have low albedo (absorbing 85 to 95%). Albedo differences create feedback loops in climate change: as ice and snow melt due to warming, lower-albedo ocean and land surfaces are exposed, absorbing more solar radiation and amplifying warming. This ice-albedo feedback is one of the key amplifying mechanisms in Arctic climate change.

Key ideas: Solar angle determines energy per unit area; equatorial regions receive more direct radiation than polar regions. Seasons result from Earth's axial tilt, not orbital distance. Summer occurs when a hemisphere tilts toward the sun, increasing day length and solar angle. Albedo (reflectivity) determines how much solar energy surfaces absorb; ice-albedo feedback amplifies warming.

4.6 Climate Patterns Including El Nino and La Nina

Climate differs from weather: weather is short-term atmospheric conditions at a specific place and time; climate is the long-term average pattern of temperature, precipitation, and other atmospheric variables for a region, typically measured over decades. Climate is determined by latitude (distance from equator determines solar input), altitude (temperature decreases approximately 6.5 degrees C per 1000 m elevation), proximity to large water bodies (which moderate temperature extremes due to water's high specific heat), prevailing winds (which determine whether moist oceanic or dry continental air predominates), and ocean currents (which transport heat and affect coastal temperatures and precipitation).

Rain shadows illustrate how topography and prevailing winds interact to create dramatically different climates on opposite sides of mountain ranges. When moisture-laden air is forced to rise over a mountain range (orographic lifting), it cools and precipitation occurs on the windward side. The descending air on the leeward side is dry and warm (having lost its moisture), creating a rain shadow desert. The western Cascades are wet temperate rainforest; eastern Oregon and Washington in the rain shadow are semi-arid. The Andes rain shadow creates the Atacama Desert on South America's western coast.

El Nino and La Nina are opposite phases of the El Nino-Southern Oscillation (ENSO), a climate pattern resulting from periodic changes in ocean surface temperatures and atmospheric pressure in the tropical Pacific. During normal conditions, trade winds push warm surface water westward, allowing cold, nutrient-rich water to upwell along the South American coast. During El Nino (occurring approximately every 3 to 7 years), trade winds weaken, warm water spreads eastward, suppressing upwelling, reducing fisheries productivity off Peru and Ecuador, and triggering droughts in Australia and flooding in western South America. La Nina (the opposite phase) intensifies trade winds, strengthens upwelling, and tends to produce the opposite climate anomalies globally. ENSO has global climate impacts including drought in sub-Saharan Africa, flooding in the southeastern United States, and above-normal hurricane activity in the Atlantic during La Nina years.

Key ideas: Climate is long-term average atmospheric conditions; weather is short-term. Climate determined by latitude, altitude, proximity to water, prevailing winds, and ocean currents. Rain shadows form when orographic lifting deposits moisture on windward slopes, leaving leeward slopes dry. El Nino weakens trade winds, spreads warm water east, suppresses upwelling; La Nina is the opposite. Both have widespread global climate consequences.


Unit 5 – Land and Water Use

5.1 Environmental Legislation and Tragedy of the Commons

The tragedy of the commons, articulated by Garrett Hardin in 1968, describes how rational individuals acting in their own short-term interest can collectively degrade shared resources even when this outcome is bad for everyone. In a common-pool resource (a shared resource from which individuals can subtract and from which it is difficult to exclude users), each individual has an incentive to maximize their own use because they capture all the benefit but share the cost of depletion with everyone. This logic explains overgrazing on shared pastures, overfishing in international waters, and air and water pollution where individuals or companies externalize costs onto the public. Solutions include privatization (creating individual ownership and therefore individual incentive to conserve), government regulation, and community management systems with enforceable rules.

Major U.S. environmental legislation represents the governmental response to unmanaged exploitation of common resources. The National Environmental Policy Act (NEPA, 1970) requires Environmental Impact Statements for major federal actions. The Clean Air Act (1970) regulates air pollutant emissions. The Clean Water Act (1972) regulates discharge of pollutants into navigable waters and established the basic structure for water quality regulation. The Endangered Species Act (1973) provides protection for threatened and endangered species and their habitats. CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) coordinates international trade restrictions. These laws are regularly referenced on the AP Environmental Science exam in the context of specific environmental problems.

Environmental law operates through a combination of command-and-control regulations (setting specific limits on pollutant emissions or required technologies) and market-based approaches (cap-and-trade systems, pollution taxes, and subsidies for clean technologies). Cap-and-trade systems set an overall cap on emissions, distribute or auction allowances to emit, and allow companies to trade allowances, creating economic incentives to reduce emissions below the cap. Carbon pricing (through either a carbon tax or cap-and-trade) is widely regarded by economists as the most efficient approach to reducing greenhouse gas emissions by internalizing the externalized cost of climate change into the prices of fossil fuels.

Key ideas: Tragedy of the commons: shared resources are overexploited by individually rational users; solutions include regulation, private ownership, and community management. Key U.S. environmental laws: NEPA, Clean Air Act, Clean Water Act, Endangered Species Act. Command-and-control vs. market-based regulatory approaches. Cap-and-trade internalizes pollution costs through tradable emission allowances.

5.2 Forestry and Clearcutting

Forests cover approximately 31% of Earth's land surface and provide critical ecosystem services including carbon storage, water cycle regulation, biodiversity habitat, soil protection, timber and non-timber products, and climate moderation. Old-growth forests (never significantly disturbed by human activity) have complex structures with multiple canopy layers, large standing and fallen dead trees, and high biodiversity that cannot be replicated in managed plantations within human timescales. Deforestation globally continues at approximately 10 million hectares per year, driven primarily by agricultural expansion (particularly for cattle ranching and palm oil and soy production in tropical regions).

Clearcutting removes all trees from a large area in a single harvest, maximizing short-term timber yield but causing extensive environmental damage: soil erosion from loss of vegetative cover, stream sedimentation, loss of wildlife habitat (particularly for interior forest species requiring large undisturbed areas), disruption of hydrological function, and release of stored carbon. Clearcutting is efficient and economically attractive for timber companies because it reduces harvest costs, allows mechanized operations, and is well-suited to tree species (like Douglas fir and loblolly pine) that regenerate in full sun. However, replanted clearcuts are often monoculture plantations with low biodiversity and different ecological character from the original forest.

Alternative harvesting methods reduce environmental impact but typically at higher cost. Selective cutting removes only specific trees (older, diseased, or commercially valuable individuals) while leaving the forest structure largely intact. Shelterwood cutting removes trees in two or more stages, leaving seed trees to regenerate the forest. Riparian buffers (leaving uncut forest strips along streams) protect water quality by filtering runoff. Reduced-impact logging practices (careful road placement, directional felling, limiting skid trails) reduce soil disturbance. Sustainable forestry certification programs (Forest Stewardship Council) allow consumers to identify wood products from sustainably managed forests.

Key ideas: Forests provide carbon storage, water regulation, biodiversity habitat, and timber. Clearcutting maximizes short-term yield but causes erosion, habitat loss, and carbon release. Alternative methods (selective cutting, shelterwood, riparian buffers) reduce damage. Old-growth forests provide irreplaceable ecological values. Sustainable forestry certification provides market incentives for responsible management.

5.3 Agriculture and the Green Revolution

Agriculture began approximately 10,000 years ago and remains the dominant use of Earth's land surface, occupying approximately 50% of habitable land. Traditional agriculture produced relatively low yields using human and animal labor, organic amendments, and locally adapted crop varieties. The Green Revolution of the 1950s to 1970s dramatically transformed agricultural productivity through the development of high-yielding varieties (HYVs) of wheat, rice, and maize that responded strongly to fertilizer and irrigation inputs. Combined with expanded irrigation infrastructure, synthetic fertilizer production (using the Haber-Bosch process to convert atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia), and pesticide use, Green Revolution technology allowed global food production to keep pace with rapidly growing population and averted the mass famines predicted in the 1960s.

The Green Revolution produced enormous food production gains but also significant environmental costs. Heavy fertilizer use contributed to nitrogen and phosphorus loading of waterways, causing eutrophication and dead zones. Pesticide use reduced pest populations but also harmed non-target species and pollinator populations. Irrigation led to waterlogging, salinization of soils, and depletion of aquifers. The shift from diverse traditional crop varieties to a few high-yield varieties reduced genetic diversity in major crops, creating vulnerability to new pests and diseases. Dependence on fossil fuel inputs for synthetic fertilizers, pesticides, and fuel made modern agriculture vulnerable to energy price fluctuations.

Genetically modified organisms (GMOs) represent a continuation of the genetic improvement approach pioneered by the Green Revolution, using modern molecular techniques to introduce specific genes conferring herbicide resistance, pest resistance (Bt crops expressing insecticidal proteins), drought tolerance, or enhanced nutritional profiles (Golden Rice engineered to produce beta-carotene). GMO crops are widely adopted in the United States, Brazil, and Argentina but face significant regulatory restrictions and public opposition in Europe. AP Environmental Science covers both the potential benefits (reduced pesticide use in Bt crops, increased yields in drought-stressed regions) and concerns (development of herbicide-resistant weeds, potential ecological effects of gene flow to wild relatives, corporate control of the seed supply) of agricultural biotechnology.

Key ideas: Green Revolution increased yields through high-yielding varieties, synthetic fertilizers, irrigation, and pesticides. Environmental costs include eutrophication, salinization, aquifer depletion, pesticide effects on non-target species, and reduced crop genetic diversity. GMO crops extend genetic improvement but raise ecological and socioeconomic concerns. Modern agriculture depends heavily on fossil fuel inputs.

5.4 Irrigation, Pest Control, and Integrated Pest Management

Approximately 70% of global freshwater withdrawals are used for irrigation, making agriculture by far the largest user of water. Flood irrigation (releasing water onto fields and allowing it to spread) is the oldest and most common method but the least efficient, with much water lost to evaporation, runoff, and percolation beyond the root zone. Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the root zone through perforated pipes or emitters, reducing water use by 30 to 70% compared to flood irrigation. Center-pivot irrigation uses rotating sprinkler systems over large circular fields and achieves intermediate efficiency. Inefficient irrigation contributes to soil salinization when evaporating water leaves dissolved salts behind, and to aquifer depletion when withdrawal rates exceed recharge.

Pest control in conventional agriculture relies heavily on synthetic pesticides (herbicides, insecticides, and fungicides). While effective at reducing crop losses, synthetic pesticides create several problems: development of pesticide-resistant pest populations (through natural selection operating on genetic variation in the pest population), harm to non-target organisms (including pollinators, natural predators of pests, soil organisms, and aquatic life through runoff), persistence in the environment leading to bioaccumulation and biomagnification, and human health risks through occupational exposure and residues on food. The pesticide treadmill describes the escalating cycle in which resistance requires increased doses or new chemicals, creating ever-greater environmental exposure.

Integrated Pest Management (IPM) is a holistic approach that minimizes pesticide use by combining multiple pest control strategies: biological control (introducing or conserving natural predators and parasitoids), cultural control (crop rotation, timing of planting, resistant crop varieties, sanitation), physical and mechanical control (traps, barriers, hand removal), and targeted chemical control used only when monitoring indicates that pest populations exceed economic thresholds. IPM reduces environmental and health impacts of pest control while maintaining crop protection, often achieving comparable or lower crop losses than conventional pesticide-dependent approaches. AP Environmental Science frequently contrasts IPM with conventional chemical approaches and asks students to evaluate specific pest management scenarios.

Key ideas: Drip irrigation is most efficient; flood irrigation most common and least efficient. Inefficient irrigation causes salinization and aquifer depletion. Synthetic pesticides create resistance, harm non-target species, and biomagnify. IPM integrates biological, cultural, physical, and targeted chemical controls to minimize pesticide use while protecting crops.

5.5 Meat Production and Sustainable Agriculture

Meat production is significantly less resource efficient than plant food production because energy losses at each trophic transfer mean producing 1 kg of beef requires approximately 7 to 10 kg of grain (as feed), large volumes of water, and substantial land area. Livestock agriculture accounts for approximately 14.5% of global greenhouse gas emissions (including methane from ruminant digestion and manure management, and nitrous oxide from manure and feed crop fertilization), significant contributions to water pollution through animal waste runoff and nitrogen leaching, land degradation through overgrazing, and is a major driver of tropical deforestation for pasture and feed crop production. Reducing meat consumption, particularly beef, is one of the most significant individual actions for reducing environmental impact.

Factory farming (concentrated animal feeding operations, or CAFOs) raises large numbers of animals in high-density confined conditions to minimize production costs. Environmental concerns include concentrated waste production (creating air pollution from ammonia and hydrogen sulfide, and water pollution from manure lagoon leakage and runoff), antibiotic use promoting antibiotic resistance in bacteria, and animal welfare issues. Alternatives including grass-fed beef on non-arable land, integrated crop-livestock systems, and plant-based protein substitutes represent different approaches to reducing the environmental footprint of protein production.

Sustainable agriculture aims to produce food that meets present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs. Practices include crop rotation (rotating different crops in sequence to break pest and disease cycles and reduce soil nutrient depletion), cover cropping (planting non-commercial crops between growing seasons to protect soil and fix nitrogen), reduced tillage or no-till farming (leaving crop residues to protect soil, reduce erosion, and build organic matter), agroforestry (integrating trees with crops and/or livestock for multiple benefits), organic agriculture (avoiding synthetic fertilizers and pesticides), and local food systems (reducing transportation emissions). AP Environmental Science frequently asks students to evaluate these practices for their environmental benefits and tradeoffs.

Key ideas: Meat production requires more land, water, and energy than equivalent plant calories due to trophic level inefficiency. Livestock agriculture contributes approximately 14.5% of global greenhouse gas emissions and drives deforestation. Sustainable agriculture practices (crop rotation, cover cropping, no-till, agroforestry) reduce environmental impacts. Reducing beef consumption is among the highest-impact individual environmental choices.

5.6 Overfishing, Aquaculture, and Sustainable Fisheries

Overfishing occurs when fish are harvested faster than populations can reproduce, reducing stocks below levels that can sustain maximum long-term yields. Maximum sustainable yield (MSY) is the theoretical maximum catch that can be taken indefinitely without depleting the population; populations fished at or beyond MSY may collapse suddenly if environmental variability reduces recruitment below expected levels. Many of the world's major commercial fish stocks (Atlantic cod, Pacific bluefin tuna, orange roughy, Chilean sea bass) have been severely depleted or collapsed through overfishing. Bycatch, the unintended capture of non-target species (sea turtles, seabirds, dolphins, juvenile fish, and coral) in fishing operations, adds to the ecological impact of commercial fishing beyond the target species.

Causes of overfishing include open-access fisheries (the tragedy of the commons applied to fish), government subsidies that keep fishing fleets economically viable beyond sustainable catch levels, illegal, unreported, and unregulated (IUU) fishing, technological improvements in fish detection and capture efficiency, and growing global demand for seafood. Management approaches include catch limits (total allowable catch, TAC), gear restrictions (minimum mesh sizes, gear types that reduce bycatch), marine protected areas (no-take zones that allow fish populations to recover and spillover into surrounding areas), and individual transferable quotas (ITQs, which assign tradable property rights to portions of the allowable catch, creating economic incentives for sustainability).

Aquaculture (fish and shellfish farming) is the fastest-growing food sector globally and provides approximately half of global seafood. Aquaculture can relieve pressure on wild stocks, produce protein efficiently, and be highly productive per unit area. However, conventional aquaculture has significant environmental costs: concentrated waste production causing eutrophication in surrounding waters, disease and parasite transmission to wild populations, escapes of non-native or domesticated fish strains that interbreed with wild populations, habitat destruction for shrimp pond construction (mangrove removal), and use of wild-caught forage fish to produce fishmeal feed for carnivorous farmed species (salmon, tuna). Sustainable aquaculture practices including integrated multi-trophic aquaculture (IMTA, combining fish with filter feeders and seaweed to reduce waste) and plant-based feeds are being developed to reduce these impacts.

Key ideas: Overfishing depletes stocks below sustainable levels; many major fisheries have collapsed. Causes include open-access tragedy of the commons, subsidies, and technological efficiency. Management tools include catch limits, gear restrictions, marine protected areas, and individual transferable quotas. Aquaculture provides half of global seafood but has environmental costs including eutrophication, disease, and habitat destruction.

5.7 Mining and Resource Extraction

Mining extracts valuable minerals and materials from Earth's crust for use in industrial, technological, and construction applications. Surface mining (including open-pit mining, strip mining, and mountaintop removal) removes overburden to access near-surface deposits; it is more economical than underground mining but causes extensive surface disturbance, habitat destruction, erosion, and sedimentation of nearby streams. Mountaintop removal coal mining in the Appalachians has buried hundreds of miles of streams under valley fills and dramatically altered the landscape. Underground mining is less surface-disruptive but creates risks of subsidence, groundwater contamination, and worker safety hazards.

Mining generates large volumes of waste, including overburden (the rock and soil removed to access the ore), tailings (finely ground rock remaining after ore processing), and mine drainage. Acid mine drainage (AMD) is one of the most serious environmental impacts of mining: when sulfide minerals (pyrite) in tailings and mine waste are exposed to oxygen and water, sulfuric acid is produced. This acid leaches toxic metals (arsenic, cadmium, lead, mercury) from surrounding rocks, producing highly acidic, metal-contaminated water that flows into streams and groundwater. AMD persists for centuries after mining ceases and is extraordinarily difficult and expensive to remediate.

The Surface Mining Control and Reclamation Act (SMCRA, 1977) requires mining companies to restore mined land to its approximate original contour and to reclaim the site for beneficial use after mining is complete, and establishes a fund from an abandoned mine tax to reclaim legacy mining sites. Reducing the environmental impact of mining also involves improving ore processing efficiency to reduce waste volumes, developing techniques for in-place (in situ) leaching of ore without full surface or underground excavation, recycling metals to reduce primary mining demand, and developing substitute materials to reduce reliance on mined materials. As the transition to clean energy requires large quantities of lithium, cobalt, nickel, and rare earth elements for batteries and electronics, the environmental governance of mining is of increasing importance.

Key ideas: Surface mining (open-pit, strip, mountaintop removal) causes extensive habitat and stream damage. Acid mine drainage contaminates water with sulfuric acid and toxic metals for centuries. SMCRA requires reclamation of mined land. Increasing demand for battery minerals (lithium, cobalt, rare earths) for clean energy technology is driving new mining pressures.

5.8 Urbanization, Ecological Footprints, and Runoff Reduction

Urbanization, the growth of cities and the conversion of rural and natural land to urban uses, is the dominant land use trend of the 21st century; more than half of the world's population now lives in urban areas, and this proportion continues to rise. Urban development replaces permeable vegetated land with impervious surfaces (pavement, rooftops, compacted soils) that prevent rainwater infiltration, dramatically increasing surface runoff volume and velocity. Urban streams receive flashy, high-volume runoff that causes bank erosion, channel incision, and export of nutrients, sediment, and pollutants (oil, heavy metals, pathogens) to downstream waters. Urban heat islands result from the replacement of vegetation (which cools through transpiration and shading) with heat-absorbing dark surfaces and the concentration of waste heat from buildings, vehicles, and industry.

The ecological footprint is a measure of the land and water area required to produce the resources a population consumes and to absorb the wastes it generates. Global ecological footprint currently exceeds Earth's biocapacity (the amount it can sustainably provide) by approximately 70%, meaning humanity is consuming natural capital faster than it can be regenerated. High-income nations have the largest per capita footprints; the United States requires the equivalent of approximately five Earths if everyone lived as Americans do. The ecological footprint concept highlights the unsustainability of current consumption patterns and provides a framework for comparing the resource demands of different lifestyles and policies.

Low-impact development (LID) and green infrastructure approaches reduce urban runoff by mimicking natural hydrology. Green roofs (vegetated rooftops that absorb and slow runoff, reduce cooling loads, and provide habitat), rain gardens and bioswales (vegetated depressions that capture and infiltrate runoff), permeable pavements (allowing infiltration through road and parking lot surfaces), urban tree planting (intercepting rainfall, promoting infiltration, and cooling through transpiration), and retention and detention ponds (capturing runoff for gradual infiltration or controlled release) together can significantly reduce the hydrological and water quality impacts of urban development. These approaches provide multiple co-benefits including habitat, cooling, aesthetics, and carbon storage.

Key ideas: Urbanization replaces permeable surfaces with impervious cover, increasing runoff volume and velocity and causing stream degradation. Urban heat islands result from dark surfaces and waste heat. Global ecological footprint exceeds biocapacity by approximately 70%. Low-impact development (green roofs, rain gardens, permeable pavement, urban trees) reduces runoff and provides multiple co-benefits.

5.9 Sustainability and Resource Management Strategies

Sustainability requires meeting present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their needs, a definition encompassing ecological limits, social equity, and economic viability simultaneously. The three pillars of sustainability (environmental integrity, social equity, and economic viability) must all be addressed together; solutions that are ecologically sound but economically unviable or socially unjust will not be implemented at scale. Sustainability science seeks to understand the interactions among human and natural systems and to develop pathways to desirable futures within planetary boundaries.

Circular economy principles offer a framework for redesigning industrial systems to eliminate waste and continuously recycle materials. In contrast to the linear "take-make-dispose" economy, the circular economy designs products for durability, repairability, and end-of-life material recovery; uses renewable energy; minimizes waste; and keeps materials in use at their highest value for as long as possible. Implementing circular economy approaches requires redesigning products, supply chains, business models, and waste management systems simultaneously. Examples include product-as-a-service models (leasing rather than selling products, giving manufacturers incentive to design for durability), industrial symbiosis (where the waste of one industrial facility becomes the input for another), and extended producer responsibility (requiring manufacturers to take back and recycle products at end of life).

Specific resource management strategies for sustainability include the use of best available technology to improve efficiency, application of the precautionary principle (taking preventive action when scientific evidence suggests potential harm even without complete certainty), full cost accounting (including environmental and social externalities in the prices of goods and services), and setting and enforcing sustainable yield limits for renewable resources (fisheries, forests, water). AP Environmental Science expects students to evaluate specific environmental problems using these frameworks, propose solutions, and assess their feasibility, costs, and benefits across ecological, social, and economic dimensions.

Key ideas: Sustainability requires meeting present needs without compromising future capacity across ecological, social, and economic dimensions. Circular economy design eliminates waste by keeping materials in use at highest value. Resource management strategies include best available technology, precautionary principle, full cost accounting, and sustainable yield limits. Solutions must address all three sustainability pillars to be implemented at scale.


Unit 6 – Energy Resources and Consumption

6.1 Renewable and Nonrenewable Energy Resources

Energy resources are broadly classified as nonrenewable (finite stocks that are depleted by use and cannot be regenerated on human timescales) or renewable (continuously replenished by natural processes and inexhaustible on human timescales). Nonrenewable resources include fossil fuels (coal, oil, and natural gas, formed over millions of years from the remains of ancient organisms) and nuclear fuels (uranium and thorium, extracted from finite ore deposits). Renewable resources include solar, wind, hydroelectric, geothermal, and biomass energy. The distinction between renewable and nonrenewable is not absolute: hydroelectric power can be disrupted by drought; some geothermal reservoirs are locally depletable; and biomass is only renewable if harvested sustainably.

The primary energy mix describes the combination of energy sources supplying a nation's or the world's total energy needs. Globally, fossil fuels still account for approximately 80% of primary energy, with oil providing approximately 30%, coal approximately 25%, and natural gas approximately 25%. Nuclear power provides approximately 5%; renewable energy (primarily hydroelectric, wind, and solar) provides the remaining approximately 15%. However, the share of renewables has been growing rapidly as their costs have fallen dramatically. Solar photovoltaic electricity costs have declined approximately 90% over the past decade, making solar the cheapest form of new electricity generation in most of the world.

The transition from fossil fuels to renewable energy is driven by climate change concerns (reducing greenhouse gas emissions), energy security (reducing dependence on fossil fuel imports), and falling renewable energy costs that now make renewables competitive with or cheaper than fossil fuels in most markets. However, the transition faces challenges including the intermittency of solar and wind (which requires energy storage or backup generation), the need for expanded and upgraded transmission infrastructure, the geographic concentration of renewable resources, and the political and economic power of incumbent fossil fuel industries. Understanding the drivers, barriers, and progress of the energy transition is central to AP Environmental Science's treatment of energy policy.

Key ideas: Nonrenewable energy (fossil fuels, nuclear) is finite; renewable energy (solar, wind, hydro, geothermal, biomass) is continuously replenished. Fossil fuels still provide approximately 80% of global primary energy. Solar and wind costs have fallen dramatically, making renewables cost-competitive. Energy transition faces challenges of intermittency, infrastructure, and incumbent industry resistance.

6.2 Global Energy Consumption and Distribution

Global primary energy consumption is highly unequal: the United States, with approximately 4% of world population, consumes approximately 15% of global energy. High-income nations have large per capita energy footprints driven by energy-intensive industry, transport, heating and cooling, and consumer goods. Low-income nations have small per capita footprints but aspire to the material living standards of high-income nations, creating tension between development goals and climate objectives. Total global energy consumption has grown dramatically over the past century and continues to grow, driven primarily by population growth and economic development in low and middle income countries.

Energy use efficiency describes how much useful work or service is produced per unit of energy consumed. The United States uses energy approximately twice as efficiently as it did in 1970 (in terms of GDP per unit energy), due to more efficient appliances, vehicles, buildings, and industrial processes. Energy efficiency improvements represent the cheapest and fastest way to reduce energy demand and greenhouse gas emissions, because the savings in energy cost typically pay for the efficiency investment in a few years. The rebound effect partially offsets efficiency gains: when energy becomes cheaper to use per service (through efficiency improvements), consumers tend to use more of it, partially canceling the reduction in total energy use.

The energy-poverty nexus describes how access to affordable, reliable energy is essential for human development: electricity enables education (lighting for evening study), healthcare (refrigeration for vaccines, powered medical equipment), economic productivity (power for businesses and industry), and food security (irrigation pumps, refrigeration). Approximately 770 million people still lack access to electricity, most in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia. Distributed renewable energy systems (small-scale solar home systems, community microgrids) are increasingly providing first-time electricity access to rural communities in developing nations at lower cost than extending the centralized grid.

Key ideas: Global energy consumption is highly unequal; wealthy nations have much larger per capita footprints. Efficiency improvements are the cheapest and fastest way to reduce energy use. Rebound effects partially offset efficiency gains. Energy access is essential for human development; approximately 770 million people lack electricity. Distributed renewables are enabling first-time energy access in developing nations.

6.3 Fossil Fuels and Nuclear Power

Fossil fuels (coal, oil, and natural gas) formed from organic material deposited in sedimentary basins and transformed by heat and pressure over millions of years. Coal is the most abundant fossil fuel and the most carbon-intensive; it is used primarily for electricity generation and steel production. Oil is the most versatile and energy-dense liquid fuel, dominating transportation. Natural gas (primarily methane) is the least carbon-intensive fossil fuel, used for electricity generation, heating, and industrial processes. Hydraulic fracturing (fracking) has dramatically increased U.S. natural gas and oil production from shale formations, but raises concerns about groundwater contamination, methane leakage, induced seismicity, and surface disturbance.

Combustion of fossil fuels produces carbon dioxide (the primary greenhouse gas driving climate change), sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides (causing acid rain and photochemical smog), particulate matter (causing respiratory disease), mercury and other heavy metals (from coal combustion), and other pollutants. The social cost of carbon attempts to quantify the total economic damage associated with emitting one additional ton of CO2, including effects on agriculture, health, flooding, and ecosystem services. Including the social cost of carbon in energy pricing would make fossil fuels substantially more expensive than renewables in most markets, reflecting their true total cost to society.

Nuclear power generates electricity through fission of uranium-235 or plutonium-239 in a controlled chain reaction, producing heat that drives steam turbines. Nuclear power produces no direct greenhouse gas emissions during operation, operates with high capacity factors (operating nearly continuously unlike intermittent renewables), and requires very little land area per unit energy produced. The primary concerns are the risk of accidents (Three Mile Island 1979, Chernobyl 1986, Fukushima 2011), the unsolved problem of long-term storage of radioactive waste (which remains hazardous for thousands of years), high construction costs and long construction times, proliferation risks from enrichment technology, and public opposition. Advanced reactor designs including molten salt reactors and small modular reactors are being developed to address some of these concerns.

Key ideas: Coal is most carbon-intensive; natural gas least among fossil fuels. Combustion produces CO2, SO2, NOx, particulates, and mercury. The social cost of carbon quantifies total economic damage per ton CO2 emitted. Nuclear produces no direct greenhouse gases but raises concerns about accidents, waste storage, high costs, and proliferation.

6.4 Renewable Energy (Solar, Wind, Hydroelectric, Geothermal, Biomass)

Solar energy can be captured through photovoltaic (PV) cells (converting sunlight directly to electricity through the photovoltaic effect in semiconductor materials) or concentrated solar power (CSP, using mirrors or lenses to concentrate sunlight to generate heat that drives a steam turbine). Solar PV costs have declined approximately 90% since 2010, making solar the cheapest form of new electricity generation in most markets. Solar is intermittent (only generating during daylight and when not clouded), requiring storage or complementary generation. Distributed rooftop solar reduces transmission losses and democratizes energy production; utility-scale solar farms can generate at very low cost but require large land areas.

Wind energy converts kinetic energy of moving air into electricity using wind turbines. Onshore wind is the cheapest form of electricity generation in many markets; offshore wind is more expensive but benefits from stronger, more consistent winds and fewer land use conflicts. Wind is also intermittent and resource-dependent, requiring complementary generation or storage. Hydroelectric power harnesses the potential energy of water stored at elevation, driving turbines as it falls. Hydroelectric power is reliable, dispatchable (can be adjusted to meet demand), and produces no direct emissions, but large dams displace communities, block fish migration, alter downstream sediment and nutrient flows, and flood terrestrial and freshwater habitats.

Geothermal energy uses heat from Earth's interior for electricity generation and direct heating. High-temperature geothermal resources (suitable for electricity generation) are concentrated in geologically active regions (Iceland, New Zealand, Philippines, western United States). Enhanced geothermal systems (EGS) can potentially access geothermal resources more widely by injecting water into hot dry rock. Biomass energy uses organic material (wood, agricultural residues, energy crops, municipal solid waste) as fuel. Combustion of biomass releases CO2, but if biomass is grown sustainably and regrows to reabsorb the CO2, the net carbon balance can be approximately neutral. However, land use competition with food production, potential deforestation, and air quality impacts from combustion are significant concerns for large-scale biomass energy.

Key ideas: Solar PV costs have declined approximately 90% since 2010, now cheapest new electricity source in most markets. Wind is cheapest or second cheapest new electricity in many markets. Both solar and wind are intermittent, requiring storage or backup. Hydroelectric is reliable and dispatchable but disrupts rivers. Geothermal is reliable but geographically limited. Biomass can be approximately carbon-neutral if sustainably managed but competes with food production.

6.5 Hydrogen Fuel Cells and Alternative Energy

Hydrogen fuel cells generate electricity through an electrochemical reaction between hydrogen and oxygen, producing only water as a byproduct. Fuel cells are more efficient than combustion engines (converting up to 60% of hydrogen's energy to electricity, compared to approximately 25% for internal combustion engines), operate quietly, and produce zero direct emissions when using pure hydrogen. Hydrogen is not a primary energy source but an energy carrier: it must be produced from another energy source and then converted back to electricity or heat in the fuel cell. Green hydrogen is produced by electrolysis of water using renewable electricity (zero-carbon pathway but currently expensive); blue hydrogen is produced from natural gas with carbon capture and storage; grey hydrogen is produced from natural gas without carbon capture (currently the cheapest and most common production method but not low-carbon).

The hydrogen economy describes a potential future energy system in which hydrogen plays a central role in storing and distributing energy, particularly for applications where direct electrification is difficult: long-distance shipping, aviation, steel production, and long-duration energy storage. The challenges include the high cost of green hydrogen production (currently 3 to 5 times more expensive than fossil fuel alternatives), the lack of hydrogen distribution infrastructure, storage difficulties (hydrogen must be compressed or liquefied for practical storage), and the energy inefficiency of the full hydrogen chain compared to direct electrification (electricity to hydrogen to electricity loses approximately two-thirds of the original energy).

Other alternative energy technologies under development include tidal and wave energy (capturing the kinetic and potential energy of ocean water movement), ocean thermal energy conversion (exploiting temperature differences between surface and deep ocean water), and advanced nuclear technologies (small modular reactors, thorium reactors, and ultimately nuclear fusion, which would use hydrogen isotopes to generate energy with minimal radioactive waste). While many of these technologies are promising, they currently play a very small role in the global energy mix and face significant technical, economic, and regulatory challenges before they could be deployed at scale.

Key ideas: Hydrogen fuel cells produce electricity and water through electrochemical hydrogen-oxygen reaction. Hydrogen is an energy carrier, not a primary source; green hydrogen uses renewable electricity for electrolysis. Hydrogen economy faces cost, infrastructure, storage, and efficiency challenges. Wave, tidal, and advanced nuclear technologies are under development but currently minor contributors.

6.6 Energy Conservation and Efficiency

Energy conservation (using less energy by reducing services or changing behavior) and energy efficiency (providing the same or more services using less energy through improved technology and design) are the most cost-effective strategies for reducing energy demand, greenhouse gas emissions, and environmental impacts. Buildings account for approximately 40% of total U.S. energy consumption; industrial processes account for approximately 30%; and transportation for approximately 30%. Significant efficiency improvements are achievable in all three sectors through available technologies.

In buildings, the most effective efficiency measures include increased insulation, high-performance windows, air sealing to reduce infiltration, highly efficient heating and cooling systems (heat pumps are 3 to 4 times more efficient than electric resistance heating), LED lighting (75% more efficient than incandescent), and Energy Star appliances. Net-zero buildings produce as much energy as they consume annually through on-site renewable generation combined with high efficiency. Green building rating systems (LEED, ENERGY STAR buildings) provide standards and incentives for efficient building design and operation.

In transportation, fuel economy standards (CAFE standards in the United States) require manufacturers to meet minimum average fuel efficiency for their fleets. Electric vehicles (EVs) are approximately 3 to 4 times more efficient than internal combustion engine vehicles in converting stored energy to motion. Even when EVs are charged with the current U.S. electricity grid (which is approximately 40% fossil fuel), they produce lower total lifecycle greenhouse gas emissions than average gasoline vehicles; as the grid becomes cleaner, EVs' carbon advantages grow. Public transit, walking, cycling, and land use planning that reduces travel distances (dense, mixed-use development with transit access) are also important components of a sustainable transportation system. AP Environmental Science expects students to calculate energy use, compare efficiency of different transportation and energy systems, and evaluate policies for reducing energy demand.

Key ideas: Buildings (40%), industry (30%), and transportation (30%) each represent large efficiency opportunities. High-efficiency building envelope and systems, LED lighting, and heat pumps dramatically reduce building energy use. EVs are 3 to 4 times more efficient than combustion engine vehicles and produce lower lifecycle emissions even with current grids. CAFE standards, building codes, and appliance standards are regulatory tools for improving efficiency.


Unit 7 – Atmospheric Pollution

7.1 Introduction to Air Pollution and Sources

Air pollution consists of substances in the atmosphere at concentrations sufficient to harm human health, ecosystems, materials, or climate. Criteria pollutants are the six common air pollutants regulated under the Clean Air Act for which National Ambient Air Quality Standards (NAAQS) are established: particulate matter (PM2.5 and PM10), ground-level ozone, carbon monoxide (CO), sulfur dioxide (SO2), nitrogen dioxide (NO2), and lead. These pollutants come from both mobile sources (vehicles: combustion engines producing NOx, CO, VOCs, and particulates) and stationary sources (power plants and industrial facilities producing SO2, NOx, and particulates; refineries and chemical plants releasing VOCs and hazardous air pollutants).

Primary pollutants are emitted directly from sources: SO2 from coal combustion, CO from incomplete combustion, NO from high-temperature combustion, lead from leaded gasoline (largely phased out), particulate matter from combustion and industrial processes. Secondary pollutants form in the atmosphere through chemical reactions among primary pollutants or between primary pollutants and natural atmospheric constituents: ground-level ozone (formed from NOx and VOCs in sunlight), sulfuric acid (from SO2 oxidation, contributing to acid rain), nitric acid (from NOx oxidation, also contributing to acid rain), and fine particulate matter (formed from gas-phase precursors including SO2 and NH3).

The 1970 Clean Air Act and its subsequent amendments established the framework for U.S. air quality regulation. NAAQS set health-based standards for criteria pollutants. New Source Performance Standards (NSPS) require new industrial facilities to install best available technology controls. The Acid Rain Program used a cap-and-trade system to dramatically reduce SO2 and NOx emissions from power plants. Mobile source standards set emission limits for vehicles. These regulations have substantially improved U.S. air quality since 1970: emissions of all six criteria pollutants have declined significantly even as economic output, vehicle miles traveled, and energy consumption have increased.

Key ideas: Six criteria pollutants are regulated under the Clean Air Act NAAQS. Primary pollutants are directly emitted; secondary pollutants form through atmospheric chemical reactions. Mobile and stationary sources are the major categories. Clean Air Act regulations have significantly reduced U.S. criteria pollutant emissions since 1970 despite economic and population growth.

7.2 Photochemical Smog and Thermal Inversion

Photochemical smog is a type of air pollution formed through ultraviolet light-driven reactions among nitrogen oxides (NOx) and volatile organic compounds (VOCs) emitted primarily by vehicle exhaust and industrial sources. The chain of reactions begins when sunlight splits NO2 into NO and an oxygen atom, which combines with O2 to form ozone (O3). Ozone is a powerful oxidant that irritates lung tissue, impairs respiratory function, damages crops and vegetation, and degrades rubber and other materials. Photochemical smog also contains a complex mixture of other oxidants (peroxides, aldehydes, peroxyacetyl nitrates) collectively causing the hazy brown appearance and eye irritation characteristic of smog-affected cities like Los Angeles and Mexico City.

Formation of photochemical smog requires: NOx and VOC emissions, sunlight, warm temperatures, and calm wind conditions that allow pollutants to accumulate. Cities in sunny, warm climates in topographic basins (Los Angeles, Mexico City, Denver, Santiago) are most prone to severe smog because these conditions are frequently met. Reducing smog requires reducing NOx and VOC emissions through vehicle emission controls (catalytic converters, which oxidize CO and hydrocarbons and reduce NOx), vehicle fuel economy standards, use of low-VOC paints and consumer products, and clean fuel standards.

Thermal inversion is an atmospheric condition in which a layer of warm air traps cooler air (and its pollutants) below it near the surface, preventing the normal convective mixing that disperses pollutants upward. Under normal conditions, air temperature decreases with altitude, causing warm air near the surface to rise and mix with cooler air above, dispersing pollutants. During a thermal inversion, a warmer air layer acts as a lid over a cooler layer, trapping pollutants near ground level and allowing concentrations to build. Inversions are most common at night and in the morning (radiation inversions), in valleys (cold air drainage), and under anticyclonic high-pressure systems (subsidence inversions). Los Angeles sits in a basin frequently experiencing inversions, exacerbating its photochemical smog problem.

Key ideas: Photochemical smog forms when NOx and VOCs react in sunlight to produce ozone and other oxidants. Conditions: sunny, warm, still, VOC and NOx sources. Catalytic converters reduce vehicle NOx, CO, and hydrocarbon emissions. Thermal inversions trap pollutants by preventing vertical mixing. Basins and valleys are especially vulnerable to inversion-exacerbated pollution events.

7.3 Atmospheric CO2, Particulates, and Indoor Air Pollution

Carbon dioxide (CO2) is not classified as a criteria pollutant under the Clean Air Act's traditional framework but is regulated as a greenhouse gas under the Clean Air Act following the Supreme Court's 2007 ruling in Massachusetts v. EPA, which found that greenhouse gases are air pollutants under the Act's definition. Atmospheric CO2 concentration has risen from approximately 280 parts per million (ppm) before the Industrial Revolution to over 420 ppm today, primarily due to fossil fuel combustion and deforestation. This increase is the primary driver of human-caused climate change and is discussed in more detail in Unit 9.

Particulate matter (PM) is a complex mixture of solid and liquid particles suspended in air, classified by size as PM10 (particles less than 10 micrometers in diameter, which can enter the upper respiratory tract) and PM2.5 (fine particles less than 2.5 micrometers in diameter, which can penetrate deep into the lungs and enter the bloodstream). PM2.5 is the most harmful component of air pollution for human health, linked to respiratory disease, cardiovascular disease, stroke, lung cancer, and premature death. Sources include combustion (vehicles, power plants, wood burning, wildfires), industrial processes, and secondary formation from gas-phase precursors. PM pollution causes millions of premature deaths globally each year, making it the largest environmental health risk factor.

Indoor air pollution is a significant problem, particularly in low-income nations where solid fuels (wood, charcoal, crop residues, dung) are burned in open fires or simple stoves inside poorly ventilated homes for cooking and heating. Indoor biomass combustion produces high concentrations of particulates, carbon monoxide, and other pollutants that disproportionately affect women and children who spend more time near the cooking fire. In developed nations, indoor air pollutants of concern include radon (a radioactive gas produced by uranium decay in soils and rocks that enters buildings through foundations and is the second leading cause of lung cancer after smoking), volatile organic compounds (VOCs) from building materials, furniture, and consumer products, mold and biological pollutants, and secondhand tobacco smoke.

Key ideas: Atmospheric CO2 has risen from 280 to over 420 ppm due to fossil fuels and deforestation; now regulated under the Clean Air Act. PM2.5 is the most harmful air pollutant for health, causing respiratory and cardiovascular disease and premature death. Indoor air pollution from biomass burning is a major health problem in developing nations. Radon is the second leading cause of lung cancer in developed nations.

7.4 Acid Rain and Noise Pollution

Acid rain (more precisely called acid deposition) forms when sulfur dioxide (SO2) and nitrogen oxides (NOx), primarily from coal-burning power plants and vehicle exhaust, react with water vapor and oxygen in the atmosphere to form sulfuric acid and nitric acid. These acids dissolve in precipitation and fall as acid rain, snow, or fog, and also deposit as dry particles on surfaces. Normal rain has a pH of approximately 5.6 (slightly acidic due to dissolved CO2 forming carbonic acid); acid rain typically has a pH of 4 to 5, and severe episodes have been recorded below pH 2. Acid deposition is a transboundary problem because SO2 and NOx can be transported hundreds of miles downwind before depositing.

Ecological impacts of acid deposition include: acidification of lakes and streams (killing fish, amphibians, and aquatic invertebrates; the Adirondack Mountains in New York lost hundreds of fish populations to acid rain before controls were implemented), leaching of calcium and other nutrients from soils (reducing forest productivity and weakening trees against pests and drought), dissolution of buffering minerals in poorly buffered soils and waters (leaving them vulnerable to further acidification), and damage to forests (particularly high-elevation forests bathed in acid fog). Buildings, sculptures, and infrastructure (particularly limestone, marble, and metals) are also corroded by acid deposition.

Noise pollution is the presence of unwanted or harmful sound in the environment. Sources include transportation (road traffic, aircraft, trains), industrial operations, construction, and recreational activities. Noise affects human health through hearing loss (chronic exposure above approximately 85 dB), cardiovascular effects (stress response, elevated blood pressure), sleep disruption, reduced cognitive performance in children, and psychological stress. Ecological effects include disruption of animal communication (bird song masked by traffic noise, whale communication masked by ship propellers), altered predator-prey dynamics, and behavioral changes in wildlife using acoustic cues for navigation, mate selection, and territory defense. Solutions include noise barriers, land use planning separating noisy uses from residential areas, quieter engine and equipment design, and noise standards for vehicles and aircraft.

Key ideas: Acid rain forms from SO2 and NOx reacting with atmospheric water to produce sulfuric and nitric acids. Impacts include lake and stream acidification, forest damage, soil nutrient leaching, and building corrosion. Acid deposition is transboundary. Noise pollution causes hearing loss, cardiovascular effects, and ecological disruption to animal communication and behavior.

7.5 Reduction and Prevention of Air Pollutants

Reducing air pollution requires addressing emissions at the source through a combination of technology requirements, fuel standards, and regulatory limits. For stationary sources, the primary control technologies include flue gas desulfurization (scrubbers that remove SO2 by reacting it with lime or limestone before combustion gases are released), selective catalytic reduction (SCR, using a catalyst and ammonia to convert NOx to harmless N2 and water), electrostatic precipitators and fabric filters (baghouses) to capture particulate matter, and activated carbon injection to capture mercury. Power plants burning coal are required to use these technologies under New Source Performance Standards and state implementation plans.

For mobile sources, catalytic converters are the primary control technology: a three-way catalyst oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons to CO2 and water, and reduces NOx to N2 simultaneously. Catalytic converters require low-sulfur fuel (sulfur poisons the catalyst), which has driven fuel sulfur content reductions globally. Vehicle emission standards have tightened significantly over decades; California's stricter standards (permitted under the Clean Air Act and adopted by many other states) have driven technological innovation. The transition to electric vehicles eliminates tailpipe emissions entirely, addressing both urban air quality and climate concerns simultaneously.

The cap-and-trade approach was used successfully for the U.S. Acid Rain Program under Title IV of the 1990 Clean Air Act amendments: SO2 allowances were allocated to power plants, a declining annual cap was set, and plants could buy or sell allowances. The program achieved greater-than-required SO2 reductions at costs significantly below initial industry estimates, demonstrating the economic efficiency of market-based approaches to pollution control. International cooperation through the United Nations Economic Commission for Europe's Convention on Long-range Transboundary Air Pollution (CLRTAP) coordinates European acid rain reduction efforts. The Montreal Protocol's success in phasing out ozone-depleting substances demonstrates that effective international cooperation on atmospheric problems is achievable.

Key ideas: Stationary source controls: scrubbers (SO2), SCR (NOx), baghouses (particulates), carbon injection (mercury). Mobile source controls: catalytic converters (CO, hydrocarbons, NOx), low-sulfur fuel standards, vehicle emission standards, electric vehicles. Cap-and-trade effectively reduced SO2 under the Acid Rain Program. International cooperation (CLRTAP, Montreal Protocol) addresses transboundary air pollution.


Unit 8 – Aquatic and Terrestrial Pollution

8.1 Sources of Pollution and Human Impacts on Ecosystems

Pollution is the introduction of harmful substances or energy into the environment at concentrations or levels sufficient to cause damage to organisms, ecosystems, or human health. Pollution sources are classified as point sources (identifiable, discrete sources with specific discharge locations, such as a factory effluent pipe or a sewage treatment plant outfall) or nonpoint sources (diffuse sources without a single identifiable discharge point, such as agricultural runoff, urban stormwater, or atmospheric deposition). Point sources are easier to regulate and monitor; nonpoint sources are the largest contributors to water quality problems in the United States and globally.

Human activities that most significantly impact ecosystems include agriculture (nutrient and pesticide runoff, soil erosion, irrigation water withdrawals, habitat conversion), urbanization (impervious surface runoff, sewage, industrial discharges, heat island effects, habitat fragmentation), industrial activity (air and water pollutant emissions, toxic waste generation, resource extraction), mining (acid mine drainage, habitat destruction, sediment loading), transportation (air pollution from vehicle exhaust, oil spills, road and rail infrastructure fragmenting habitats), and waste management (landfill leachate, incinerator emissions, improper waste disposal).

Cumulative impacts occur when multiple stressors affect an ecosystem simultaneously, producing effects greater than the sum of individual stressors alone. A stream degraded by agricultural nutrient loading, channelization, riparian buffer removal, and thermal pollution from clearing is more severely impaired than any single stressor would produce independently. Ecosystem resilience (the capacity to absorb disturbance and recover its original structure and function) decreases as cumulative stresses accumulate, lowering the threshold at which the ecosystem shifts to an alternative, degraded state. Understanding cumulative impacts is essential for designing effective environmental management strategies and for evaluating environmental impact assessments.

Key ideas: Point sources have identifiable discharge locations and are more easily regulated; nonpoint sources are diffuse and harder to control, representing the largest water quality problem. Human activities causing ecosystem impact include agriculture, urbanization, industry, mining, and transportation. Cumulative impacts from multiple stressors reduce ecosystem resilience and can trigger threshold shifts to degraded states.

8.2 Wetlands, Mangroves, and Ecosystem Damage

Wetlands are transitional ecosystems between aquatic and terrestrial systems, characterized by soils saturated with water for all or part of the year and supporting hydrophytic (water-tolerant) vegetation. Freshwater wetlands include marshes (dominated by emergent herbaceous plants like cattails and bulrushes), swamps (dominated by trees and shrubs, flooded seasonally or permanently), bogs (fed by precipitation, acidic, nutrient-poor, dominated by sphagnum moss), and fens (fed by groundwater, less acidic, more nutrient-rich). Wetlands provide critical ecosystem services including flood attenuation (absorbing and slowly releasing floodwaters), water quality improvement (filtering nutrients and sediments), carbon storage (particularly peatlands, which store more carbon per unit area than any other ecosystem), and habitat for waterfowl, amphibians, fish, and many other species.

Mangroves are coastal wetland ecosystems dominated by salt-tolerant trees and shrubs growing in intertidal zones of tropical and subtropical coastlines. Mangroves provide habitat for juvenile fish and invertebrates (serving as nursery areas for commercially important fisheries), protect coastlines from erosion and storm waves, sequester substantial amounts of carbon in their root systems and soils, and support diverse communities of birds and other wildlife. Mangrove forests have been severely degraded globally: approximately 35 to 50% of mangrove area has been lost over the past 50 years, primarily due to conversion to shrimp aquaculture ponds, coastal development, and hydrological alteration.

Damage to wetland and mangrove ecosystems has numerous downstream consequences. Loss of coastal wetlands increases storm surge damage to coastal communities, as evidenced by the amplified damage from Hurricane Katrina in New Orleans where upstream freshwater marsh loss had increased coastal vulnerability. Loss of floodplain wetlands increases downstream flood peaks. Loss of nutrient-filtering wetlands increases aquatic nutrient loading, contributing to eutrophication. Peatland drainage and degradation releases enormous amounts of stored carbon, contributing to climate change. Restoration of wetlands and mangroves provides multiple co-benefits and is increasingly recognized as a nature-based climate solution.

Key ideas: Wetlands provide flood attenuation, water quality improvement, carbon storage, and biodiversity habitat. Mangroves protect coastlines, support fisheries, and sequester carbon. Both have been extensively degraded by agriculture, aquaculture, and development. Wetland loss increases flooding, eutrophication, and carbon emissions. Restoration provides multiple co-benefits.

8.3 Eutrophication and Thermal Pollution

Eutrophication is the process by which excess nutrients (primarily nitrogen and phosphorus) stimulate algae and cyanobacteria growth in aquatic ecosystems, degrading water quality and ecosystem function. In natural oligotrophic (nutrient-poor) lakes and coastal waters, algal growth is limited by nutrient availability, producing clear, oxygen-rich water supporting diverse biota. When nutrients increase through agricultural runoff, urban stormwater, and sewage discharge, algal blooms develop rapidly, covering the water surface, blocking light to submerged vegetation, and ultimately dying and decomposing. Bacterial decomposition of the algal biomass consumes dissolved oxygen, creating hypoxic (low oxygen) or anoxic (no oxygen) zones in which fish, invertebrates, and other aerobic organisms cannot survive.

The Gulf of Mexico hypoxic zone (often called the Dead Zone), forming each summer downstream of the Mississippi River, is the most extensively documented example of eutrophication-driven hypoxia. Nitrogen and phosphorus from agricultural fertilizers applied throughout the Mississippi River basin flow to the Gulf, where spring algal blooms create the conditions for summer oxygen depletion. The zone typically covers thousands of square kilometers, devastating bottom-dwelling fish and invertebrate communities. Similar dead zones exist in the Chesapeake Bay, the Baltic Sea, the Black Sea, and hundreds of other coastal and estuarine systems globally. Reducing agricultural nutrient runoff through improved fertilizer management (precision agriculture, timing of application), cover crops, constructed wetland buffers, and improved water treatment is essential to addressing eutrophication.

Thermal pollution occurs when industrial facilities (primarily power plants) discharge cooling water at elevated temperatures into natural water bodies. Power plants use water to condense steam back to liquid after it drives turbines, then discharge this heated water. Temperature increases of even a few degrees reduce dissolved oxygen (warm water holds less dissolved oxygen than cold water), accelerate the metabolic rates of aquatic organisms beyond their thermal tolerance, facilitate the spread of warm-water pathogens and invasive species, and can kill cold-water species (trout, salmon, and their invertebrate prey are particularly sensitive). Cooling towers (dissipating heat to the atmosphere rather than to water bodies) and thermal plume monitoring are the primary regulatory responses.

Key ideas: Eutrophication: excess nutrients stimulate algal growth, blocking light; decomposition of algae depletes dissolved oxygen, creating hypoxic dead zones. The Gulf of Mexico dead zone results from Mississippi River agricultural nutrient loading. Solutions include precision fertilizer management, cover crops, wetland buffers, and improved sewage treatment. Thermal pollution reduces dissolved oxygen and stresses cold-water species; cooling towers mitigate impacts.

8.4 Persistent Organic Pollutants and Endocrine Disruptors

Persistent organic pollutants (POPs) are synthetic organic chemicals that are resistant to environmental degradation (through chemical, biological, and photolytic processes), persist in the environment for years to decades, accumulate in fatty tissues of organisms (bioaccumulate), and magnify in concentration through food chains (biomagnify). The Stockholm Convention on Persistent Organic Pollutants (2001) identified 12 initial POPs (the "dirty dozen") including DDT (used as a pesticide), PCBs (used as electrical insulators and industrial fluids), dioxins and furans (byproducts of combustion and industrial processes), and several other chlorinated pesticides. These compounds cause a range of health effects including cancer, reproductive disruption, immune dysfunction, and neurological damage.

DDT became the most famous example of POP problems after Rachel Carson documented its effects in Silent Spring (1962). DDT was highly effective at controlling malaria-carrying mosquitoes and agricultural pests, but accumulated in the fatty tissues of birds, where it interfered with calcium metabolism and caused eggshell thinning that led to reproductive failure. Populations of bald eagles, ospreys, peregrine falcons, and brown pelicans collapsed as a result. After DDT was banned in the United States in 1972, these populations recovered dramatically, demonstrating both the harm done by POPs and the capacity of populations to recover when the chemical pressure is removed.

Endocrine disrupting chemicals (EDCs) interfere with the body's hormone system by mimicking, blocking, or interfering with natural hormones, potentially causing adverse health effects at very low concentrations. EDCs include some POPs (PCBs, DDT), some plasticizers (bisphenol A, BPA, mimicking estrogen), certain pesticides, some pharmaceuticals, and industrial chemicals. Effects on wildlife include feminization of male fish in waterways contaminated with estrogen from birth control pills and industrial effluents, intersex characteristics in frogs and other amphibians, and reproductive abnormalities in numerous species. The effects of EDCs on human health are an active area of research with significant implications for chemical regulation policy.

Key ideas: POPs are resistant to degradation, bioaccumulate in fat, and biomagnify through food chains. Stockholm Convention regulates 12+ POPs including DDT, PCBs, dioxins, and furans. DDT caused eggshell thinning and raptor population collapse; its ban allowed population recovery. Endocrine disruptors (BPA, PCBs, estrogen) interfere with hormone systems at low concentrations, causing reproductive effects in wildlife and potentially humans.

8.5 Bioaccumulation and Biomagnification

Bioaccumulation describes the process by which a substance accumulates in an organism's tissues to concentrations higher than those in the surrounding environment. Lipophilic (fat-soluble) compounds including POPs, methylmercury, and many pesticides are readily absorbed from food and water and concentrate in fatty tissues, but are eliminated from the body slowly because they are poorly water-soluble and therefore cannot be efficiently excreted. Each organism absorbs contaminants from its food throughout its lifetime, building up concentrations many times higher than those found in the abiotic environment. Bioaccumulation is measured by the bioaccumulation factor (BAF), the ratio of concentration in the organism to concentration in the environment.

Biomagnification occurs when bioaccumulated contaminants increase in concentration at each successive trophic level. Because organisms at higher trophic levels consume large quantities of lower-trophic organisms over their lifetimes, and because the contaminants in each prey item are retained while the bulk of the prey's biomass is metabolized, the contaminant concentration increases with each trophic transfer. A typical biomagnification factor is approximately 10x per trophic level for highly persistent lipophilic contaminants, meaning concentrations can be millions of times higher in top predators than in water or sediment. Top predators including polar bears, killer whales, bald eagles, and large predatory fish carry the highest body burdens.

The practical consequences of biomagnification affect human health through seafood consumption: PCBs and methylmercury are concentrated in large, long-lived predatory fish (swordfish, king mackerel, shark, tilefish, and large tuna), leading public health advisories to recommend that pregnant women, nursing mothers, and young children limit consumption of these species. Mercury is of particular concern because it is released to the atmosphere by coal combustion and volcanic activity, undergoes methylation by anaerobic bacteria in aquatic sediments, and then biomagnifies through aquatic food chains. AP Environmental Science regularly asks students to interpret bioaccumulation and biomagnification data, predict which organisms carry the highest contaminant burdens, and explain the mechanism.

Key ideas: Bioaccumulation: organisms accumulate lipophilic contaminants in fat tissue to concentrations above environmental levels. Biomagnification: concentrations increase approximately 10-fold per trophic level, so top predators have the highest burdens. Methylmercury, PCBs, and DDT/DDE biomagnify most severely. Health advisories for predatory fish consumption (pregnant women, children) reflect biomagnification risk.

8.6 Solid Waste Disposal and Waste Reduction

Municipal solid waste (MSW) includes everyday materials discarded by households and businesses: food scraps, paper, plastic, glass, metals, yard trimmings, and other materials. The United States generates approximately 250 to 300 million tons of MSW per year, averaging approximately 4.5 pounds per person per day, among the highest per capita rates globally. The waste management hierarchy prioritizes: reduce (using less material in the first place), reuse (using items multiple times before discarding), recycle (recovering material for new products), compost (organic waste), energy recovery (waste-to-energy incineration), and, lastly, disposal in landfills. Moving up the hierarchy reduces environmental impact.

Modern sanitary landfills are engineered to minimize environmental harm compared to open dumps: lined with clay and synthetic membranes to prevent leachate (liquid containing dissolved and suspended pollutants) from contaminating groundwater, equipped with leachate collection and treatment systems, and outfitted with landfill gas collection systems that capture methane (produced by anaerobic decomposition of organic waste in landfills) for energy recovery or flaring. However, even well-engineered landfills eventually fail as liners degrade, and landfill gases (methane is a potent greenhouse gas if not captured) contribute to climate change. Reducing the volume of waste sent to landfills through the 3Rs is the most effective long-term strategy.

Waste-to-energy (WtE) facilities combust non-recyclable waste to generate electricity, reducing landfill volume and recovering energy value. WtE generates air pollutants (including dioxins and furans if combustion temperatures are insufficient or flue gas treatment is inadequate) and produces ash requiring disposal. Extended producer responsibility (EPR) programs require manufacturers to take back products at end of life, creating economic incentives to design products for durability, repairability, and material recovery. Zero waste initiatives aim to divert 90% or more of waste from landfills through aggressive recycling, composting, and waste reduction programs. Composting food and yard waste (approximately 30% of MSW) reduces landfill methane emissions and produces useful soil amendment.

Key ideas: Waste management hierarchy: reduce, reuse, recycle, compost, energy recovery, then landfill. Modern landfills have liners, leachate collection, and gas capture. Landfill methane is a potent greenhouse gas if not captured. WtE reduces volume but produces air pollutants. Extended producer responsibility gives manufacturers incentives to design for material recovery. Zero waste programs aim to divert 90% from landfills.

8.7 Sewage Treatment and Water Pollution Control

Wastewater treatment removes contaminants from water before it is released to natural water bodies or reused. Primary treatment uses physical processes (screening to remove large debris, sedimentation in settling tanks to remove suspended solids) to remove approximately 25 to 35% of the biological oxygen demand (BOD) and 50 to 70% of suspended solids. Secondary treatment uses biological processes (aerobic bacteria in activated sludge tanks or trickling filters) to further remove dissolved and suspended organic material, achieving removal of approximately 85 to 95% of BOD and suspended solids. Tertiary treatment uses advanced physical, chemical, or biological processes to remove specific remaining contaminants: nitrogen and phosphorus (through biological nutrient removal or chemical precipitation), pathogens (chlorination, UV disinfection, ozonation), pharmaceuticals, and endocrine disruptors.

Sewage sludge (biosolids) remaining after treatment can be treated and applied to agricultural land as a fertilizer and soil amendment (beneficial reuse of nutrients) or incinerated or landfilled (less desirable from a resource recovery perspective). Biosolid land application is regulated by EPA's Part 503 rule, which sets concentration limits for heavy metals and pathogens. Concentrated animal feeding operations (CAFOs) generate large volumes of liquid manure stored in lagoons and applied to fields; manure lagoon failures have caused catastrophic water quality events.

The Safe Drinking Water Act regulates public water systems, setting maximum contaminant levels (MCLs) for pathogens, chemicals, and physical parameters. Drinking water treatment typically includes coagulation and flocculation (adding alum or ferric chloride to aggregate fine particles and colloids), sedimentation, filtration (through sand and gravel beds, and often activated carbon for organic chemical removal), and disinfection (chlorination is most common; UV and ozone are alternatives). Emerging contaminants including PFAS (per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances, used in nonstick coatings and firefighting foams) and pharmaceuticals present new challenges for drinking water regulation because they are widespread, persistent, and present at very low concentrations that existing treatment may not fully address.

Key ideas: Primary treatment: physical removal of solids. Secondary treatment: biological removal of dissolved organics. Tertiary treatment: removal of nutrients, pathogens, and specific contaminants. Biosolids can be land-applied with regulatory controls. Drinking water treatment includes coagulation, sedimentation, filtration, and disinfection. PFAS and pharmaceuticals are emerging contaminants challenging existing treatment.

8.8 Toxicity (LD50, Dose-Response Curve)

Toxicology is the study of adverse effects of chemicals on living organisms. The dose-response relationship is foundational: the biological effect of a chemical depends on the dose (amount of exposure), and this relationship is characterized through dose-response curves that plot the degree of biological response against the dose. A typical dose-response curve is sigmoidal (S-shaped): at very low doses, no effect is observed; as dose increases, effects begin and increase steeply; at very high doses, the maximum effect is reached and the curve plateaus.

The LD50 (lethal dose 50) is the dose of a substance required to kill 50% of a test population under specified conditions, used as a standardized measure of acute toxicity. A lower LD50 means a more toxic substance: the LD50 of botulinum toxin is approximately 1 to 2 nanograms per kilogram (extremely toxic), while the LD50 of table salt is approximately 3,000 milligrams per kilogram (essentially non-toxic at normal exposures). The LC50 is the equivalent measure for airborne concentrations. LD50 values allow comparison of relative toxicities of different substances and regulatory determination of safe exposure levels, typically set at a fraction (often 1/100 or 1/1000) of the no-observable-adverse-effect level (NOAEL) determined in animal studies.

The threshold dose is the dose below which no adverse effect is observed; below this level, an organism can detoxify and repair itself faster than the chemical causes damage. However, some substances (particularly carcinogens, mutagens, and endocrine disruptors) may have no safe threshold: even very small exposures may contribute to risk. The precautionary principle argues that in the absence of certain evidence that a substance is safe, regulatory action should be taken to reduce exposure when plausible harm is suspected. Synergism occurs when the combined toxicity of two substances is greater than the sum of their individual toxicities; antagonism occurs when substances reduce each other's effects. These interactions complicate risk assessment for complex chemical mixtures.

Key ideas: Dose-response curves describe the relationship between dose and biological effect; typically sigmoidal. LD50 is the dose killing 50% of test animals; lower LD50 means greater toxicity. NOAEL establishes safe exposure levels at a fraction of the no-effect dose in animal studies. Threshold doses exist for many toxicants; carcinogens and EDCs may have no safe threshold. Synergism (combined effects greater than sum of individual effects) complicates risk assessment.

8.9 Pollution, Pathogens, and Human Health

Pollution of air, water, and soil directly threatens human health through multiple pathways. Air pollution (PM2.5, ozone, toxic air pollutants) causes respiratory disease (asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease), cardiovascular disease, stroke, lung cancer, and developmental effects in children. The World Health Organization estimates that approximately 7 million premature deaths per year are attributable to outdoor and indoor air pollution combined, making it the world's largest environmental health risk. Children are particularly vulnerable because they breathe more air per unit body weight, their developing lungs and nervous systems are more susceptible to damage, and they spend more time outdoors.

Water contamination with pathogens (disease-causing organisms including bacteria, viruses, and parasites) causes waterborne diseases including cholera, typhoid, dysentery, giardiasis, and cryptosporidiosis. Globally, approximately 1.8 million deaths per year (predominantly children under 5) are attributable to diarrheal diseases from unsafe water and sanitation. Fecal-oral transmission occurs when water contaminated with human or animal waste is used for drinking, cooking, or bathing. Cholera, caused by Vibrio cholerae, is a classic example: ingestion of contaminated water produces profuse watery diarrhea that can be fatal within hours through dehydration. The bacterium thrives in warm coastal waters and spreads rapidly in settings with inadequate water treatment and sanitation.

Chemical contamination of water supplies poses additional health risks. Lead in drinking water (primarily from lead service lines and plumbing fixtures, as in the Flint, Michigan water crisis) causes irreversible neurological damage in children, impairs cognitive development, reduces IQ, and increases behavioral problems. Arsenic, naturally occurring in groundwater in many regions (Bangladesh, India, parts of the United States), causes skin lesions, cancer (bladder, lung, skin), cardiovascular disease, and developmental effects with long-term exposure. Nitrate contamination of groundwater from agricultural fertilizers causes methemoglobinemia (blue baby syndrome) in infants, in which nitrite converted from nitrate impairs the oxygen-carrying capacity of hemoglobin. AP Environmental Science expects students to connect specific pollutants to their health effects and identify pathways of exposure.

Key ideas: Air pollution (PM2.5, ozone) causes approximately 7 million premature deaths per year globally. Waterborne pathogens cause approximately 1.8 million deaths per year, primarily children. Lead causes irreversible neurological damage in children. Arsenic in groundwater causes cancer and developmental effects. Nitrate contamination causes methemoglobinemia in infants. Children are disproportionately vulnerable to environmental pollutants.


Unit 9 – Global Change

9.1 Environmental Disasters and Ozone Depletion

Major environmental disasters have galvanized public awareness and regulatory action throughout the history of the environmental movement. The Love Canal contamination in New York (1978), where a neighborhood built on an industrial chemical waste dump experienced elevated cancer rates, birth defects, and miscarriages, led to the creation of the Superfund program (CERCLA, 1980) for cleaning up hazardous waste sites. The Bhopal disaster in India (1984), where a methyl isocyanate gas leak from a Union Carbide pesticide plant killed thousands and injured hundreds of thousands, prompted strengthened requirements for industrial chemical hazard communication and emergency planning (the Emergency Planning and Community Right-to-Know Act in the United States). The Exxon Valdez oil spill in Alaska (1989) and the Deepwater Horizon spill in the Gulf of Mexico (2010) demonstrated the devastating ecological consequences of petroleum spills and drove regulatory improvements in tanker design and offshore drilling safety.

Stratospheric ozone depletion is one of the most successful examples of identifying, understanding, and addressing a global atmospheric problem. The stratospheric ozone layer, concentrated between approximately 15 and 35 km altitude, absorbs most incoming ultraviolet-B (UV-B) radiation, which causes skin cancer, cataracts, immune suppression, and damage to aquatic ecosystems and terrestrial plants. Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), used as refrigerants, propellants, and foam-blowing agents, are chemically inert in the troposphere but are transported to the stratosphere where UV radiation breaks them down, releasing chlorine atoms that catalytically destroy ozone (each chlorine atom can destroy thousands of ozone molecules in chain reactions). The Antarctic ozone hole, first observed in the early 1980s, results from particularly efficient ozone destruction in cold stratospheric polar vortex conditions.

The Montreal Protocol (1987) is widely regarded as the most successful international environmental treaty ever negotiated. It phased out production and consumption of CFCs and other ozone-depleting substances (halons, carbon tetrachloride, methyl chloroform, hydrochlorofluorocarbons) on a schedule calibrated to the development status of signatory nations. Scientific monitoring has demonstrated that stratospheric chlorine concentrations are declining and the ozone layer is slowly recovering; the Antarctic ozone hole is projected to close by approximately 2065. The Montreal Protocol is a model for international environmental cooperation and demonstrates that nations can successfully coordinate to address shared global environmental problems when scientific consensus is clear and economically viable alternatives are available.

Key ideas: Love Canal led to Superfund; Bhopal to chemical hazard communication requirements; oil spills to tanker and drilling safety improvements. Stratospheric ozone depletion caused by CFC chlorine atoms catalytically destroying ozone; UV-B increase causes skin cancer and ecological damage. The Montreal Protocol successfully phased out CFCs; ozone layer is recovering. The Montreal Protocol is a model for international environmental cooperation.

9.2 Greenhouse Effect and Greenhouse Gases

The greenhouse effect is a natural process essential to life on Earth: without it, Earth's average temperature would be approximately negative 18 degrees C instead of the current plus 15 degrees C. Solar radiation (primarily visible light) passes through the atmosphere and is absorbed by Earth's surface, which warms and emits infrared (heat) radiation. Greenhouse gases (GHGs) in the atmosphere absorb this outgoing infrared radiation and re-emit it in all directions, including back toward Earth's surface, effectively trapping heat in the lower atmosphere. The natural greenhouse effect is driven primarily by water vapor, CO2, methane (CH4), nitrous oxide (N2O), and ozone.

The enhanced greenhouse effect refers to the amplification of natural greenhouse warming by human activities that increase GHG concentrations. The major anthropogenic greenhouse gases are carbon dioxide (CO2, produced by fossil fuel combustion, deforestation, and cement production; has a global warming potential (GWP) of 1), methane (CH4, produced by livestock digestion, rice paddies, landfills, natural gas leakage, and coal mining; GWP approximately 84 over 20 years and approximately 28 over 100 years), nitrous oxide (N2O, produced by agricultural soil management, particularly nitrogen fertilizer application, and animal waste; GWP approximately 270 over 100 years), and fluorinated gases (synthetic compounds used as refrigerants and in industrial processes; some with GWPs in the thousands to tens of thousands). CO2 is the most important anthropogenic greenhouse gas because of its enormous quantity, even though other gases have higher per-molecule warming potentials.

The relationship between GHG concentrations and temperature is well established by multiple lines of evidence: paleoclimate records (ice core data showing CO2 and temperature moving together over hundreds of thousands of years), laboratory spectroscopic measurements confirming CO2 and CH4's infrared absorption properties, satellite measurements confirming reduced infrared emission from Earth in the specific wavelengths absorbed by CO2, and observed warming patterns consistent with greenhouse gas forcing (greater warming at night than daytime, greater warming at the poles, stratospheric cooling while troposphere warms) that distinguish greenhouse warming from other potential causes of temperature change.

Key ideas: Natural greenhouse effect essential for life: GHGs trap outgoing infrared radiation. Enhanced greenhouse effect from human GHG emissions. Major GHGs: CO2 (most important by mass), CH4 (high GWP, short lifetime), N2O (high GWP, long lifetime), fluorinated gases (very high GWP). Multiple lines of evidence confirm GHG-temperature relationship: ice cores, spectroscopy, satellite measurements, and warming pattern fingerprinting.

9.3 Global Climate Change

Global average temperature has increased approximately 1.1 to 1.2 degrees C above pre-industrial (1850 to 1900) levels as of the early 2020s, and the rate of warming has accelerated: the decade from 2011 to 2020 was the warmest on record. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), which synthesizes the findings of thousands of climate scientists, states with high confidence that human-caused greenhouse gas emissions are the dominant cause of observed warming since the mid-20th century. Climate models project further warming of 1.5 to 4.5 degrees C by 2100 depending on future emission trajectories, with higher warming scenarios projected under continued heavy fossil fuel use.

Climate change impacts are widespread and already observable: rising sea levels (approximately 20 cm since 1900, accelerating; threatening coastal communities and low-lying island nations), increased frequency and intensity of heat waves, more intense precipitation events and flooding (a warmer atmosphere holds more moisture), prolonged droughts in some regions, Arctic warming at 2 to 4 times the global average rate (causing sea ice loss, permafrost thaw, and tundra shifts), changes in precipitation patterns affecting agricultural systems, glacier retreat threatening freshwater supply for hundreds of millions of people, coral bleaching and reef degradation from warming and acidification, and shifting species ranges and phenological timing disruptions.

Climate mitigation (reducing GHG emissions to limit the magnitude of warming) and adaptation (adjusting to the changes that are already inevitable) are both necessary responses. Mitigation requires rapid decarbonization of energy, industry, transportation, and agriculture through renewable energy deployment, energy efficiency, electrification, carbon capture, and land use changes. The Paris Agreement (2015) commits nations to limit warming to well below 2 degrees C above pre-industrial levels (with an aspiration of 1.5 degrees C) through nationally determined contributions (NDCs). Current national pledges are insufficient to meet the Paris goals; achieving them requires far more rapid emission reductions than are currently on track. Adaptation measures include sea wall and coastal protection construction, drought-resistant crop development, early warning systems for extreme weather events, and managed retreat from high-risk coastal and flood-prone areas.

Key ideas: Global temperature has risen approximately 1.1 to 1.2 degrees C since pre-industrial times; human GHG emissions are the dominant cause (IPCC high confidence). Projected warming: 1.5 to 4.5 degrees C by 2100 depending on emissions. Observed impacts: sea level rise, extreme heat, intense precipitation, drought, Arctic warming, coral bleaching, species range shifts. Paris Agreement targets below 2 degrees C; current pledges are insufficient.

9.4 Ocean Warming and Ocean Acidification

Oceans have absorbed approximately 90% of the excess heat trapped by enhanced greenhouse gas concentrations since pre-industrial times and approximately 25 to 30% of anthropogenic CO2 emissions. Ocean warming has increased the average sea surface temperature by approximately 0.9 degrees C since 1950 and has driven thermal expansion of seawater, contributing approximately 40% of observed sea level rise (melting ice contributes the remainder). Warming oceans are stratifying (warm surface water floating on cold deep water reduces mixing), reducing the upwelling of nutrient-rich deep water that supports fisheries in productive coastal regions. Warmer oceans intensify hurricanes and typhoons by providing more energy to these storm systems, and have caused widespread coral bleaching events as corals expel their symbiotic algae when water temperatures exceed their thermal tolerance.

Ocean acidification is a direct chemical consequence of CO2 absorption by seawater: dissolved CO2 reacts with water to form carbonic acid (H2CO3), which dissociates to release hydrogen ions (H+), reducing ocean pH. Ocean pH has declined from approximately 8.2 to 8.1 since the Industrial Revolution, a change of 0.1 pH units that represents a 26% increase in acidity (because pH is a logarithmic scale). This acidification reduces the concentration of carbonate ions (CO3 2-) that marine organisms use to build calcium carbonate shells and skeletons. Organisms most severely affected include corals, oysters, mussels, sea urchins, pteropods (sea butterflies), and many planktonic species that form the base of marine food webs. The collapse of these shell-forming organisms would have cascading effects throughout marine ecosystems and fisheries.

Coral reefs are among the most biodiverse and economically valuable marine ecosystems, supporting approximately 25% of all marine species and providing food, tourism income, and coastal protection to hundreds of millions of people. They are also among the most vulnerable to climate change. Coral bleaching occurs when water temperatures exceed the thermal tolerance of the coral's symbiotic algae (zooxanthellae) by even 1 to 2 degrees C for extended periods; the coral expels the algae (becoming white or bleached), loses its primary food source, and may die if temperatures do not return to normal quickly. Mass bleaching events have increased dramatically in frequency and severity, with the Great Barrier Reef experiencing mass bleaching in 2016, 2017, 2020, 2022, and 2024. Without rapid emission reductions, most coral reefs are projected to experience annual bleaching by mid-century.

Key ideas: Oceans have absorbed approximately 90% of excess heat and approximately 25 to 30% of CO2 from human emissions. Ocean warming intensifies hurricanes, increases stratification, and causes coral bleaching. Ocean pH has declined from 8.2 to 8.1 (26% more acidic) as dissolved CO2 forms carbonic acid. Acidification impairs shell formation in corals, mollusks, and plankton. Coral reefs face existential threat from combined warming and acidification.

9.5 Invasive and Endangered Species

Invasive species are non-native organisms introduced to an ecosystem (intentionally or accidentally) that cause ecological or economic harm. They are free from the predators, parasites, pathogens, and competitors that regulate their populations in their native range, and they may exploit resources, prey on native species, or alter habitats in ways that native species have no evolutionary defense against. The introduction pathways include deliberate introduction for agriculture, ornamental planting, or pest control; accidental transport in ship ballast water (zebra mussels in the Great Lakes), on cargo, or as stowaways in packing material; and escapes from captivity. Major invasive species impacts include the extinction or severe decline of native species, alteration of fire regimes (invasive grasses that carry fire into ecosystems where native plants lack fire adaptation), disruption of nutrient cycles, reduction in crop yields, and enormous economic costs (estimated at over $1 trillion globally per decade).

Endangered species are those facing a high risk of extinction in the wild; threatened species face a high risk of becoming endangered. Causes of endangerment include habitat loss (the primary cause globally), overexploitation (hunting, fishing, collecting), invasive species, pollution, and climate change. The Endangered Species Act (ESA, 1973) provides legal protection for species listed as endangered or threatened, prohibits taking (harming, harassing, or killing) listed species, and requires protection of critical habitat. The ESA has prevented the extinction of numerous species (bald eagle, American alligator, California condor, Florida manatee, humpback whale) and is considered one of the most effective environmental laws ever enacted, though many listed species have not yet recovered and the listing and recovery process faces political and resource constraints.

The extinction rate is currently estimated to be 100 to 1000 times higher than natural background rates, and scientists increasingly warn of a sixth mass extinction event driven by human activities. The IUCN Red List classifies species by extinction risk and currently lists over 44,000 species as threatened, including more than 40% of amphibians, more than 26% of mammals, and more than 40% of freshwater fish assessed. Biodiversity hotspots, island ecosystems, freshwater systems, and tropical forests face the greatest species loss. While individual species conservation is important, protecting large areas of intact habitat with appropriate management is the most effective long-term strategy for preventing extinction at scale.

Key ideas: Invasive species lack natural controls in their new range, causing ecological and economic damage costing over $1 trillion per decade globally. Introduction pathways include deliberate introduction, accidental transport, and escapes. The ESA has prevented extinction of many species and requires protection of listed species and critical habitat. Current extinction rates are 100 to 1000 times background rates; a sixth mass extinction is underway.

9.6 Human Impacts on Biodiversity and Global Systems

Human activities have modified more than 75% of Earth's land surface, leaving fewer than a quarter of ice-free land areas in conditions relatively unaltered by human use. This transformation has been the primary driver of biodiversity loss: habitat destruction and fragmentation eliminates the space and resources species need to persist, reduces population sizes below minimum viable thresholds, and isolates populations in fragments too small to support long-term survival. Agriculture and urban expansion continue to convert natural habitats globally, with tropical forests bearing the largest proportional losses. Restoration of degraded lands through reforestation, wetland restoration, and prairie recovery is increasingly recognized as essential alongside habitat protection.

The planetary boundaries framework identifies nine Earth system processes that together define a safe operating space for humanity within which human societies have developed over the past 12,000 years. These boundaries include climate change (CO2 concentration), biodiversity loss (extinction rate), ocean acidification, nitrogen cycle disruption, phosphorus cycle disruption, stratospheric ozone depletion, freshwater use, land system change, and chemical pollution. Scientists estimate that humanity has already exceeded safe boundaries for at least four of these processes (climate change, biodiversity loss, nitrogen cycle disruption, and land system change), and possibly more. Transgressing multiple boundaries simultaneously creates risks of abrupt and irreversible changes in Earth system function that could undermine the conditions supporting human civilization.

Addressing the intersecting crises of climate change and biodiversity loss simultaneously requires transformative changes in food systems, energy systems, land use, ocean management, and international governance. The 30x30 initiative (committing to protect 30% of land and ocean by 2030), adopted by over 100 nations at the 2022 Convention on Biological Diversity, represents a significant international commitment to halt biodiversity loss. Integrating biodiversity considerations into climate policy and climate considerations into biodiversity policy (recognizing that intact ecosystems are among the most effective carbon sinks, and that ecosystem degradation both accelerates climate change and reduces adaptation capacity) is essential for addressing both crises effectively. AP Environmental Science expects students to integrate concepts from across the course to evaluate the causes, consequences, and potential solutions to these interconnected global challenges.

Key ideas: More than 75% of Earth's land surface is modified by human activities; habitat destruction is the primary driver of biodiversity loss. Planetary boundaries framework identifies nine Earth system limits; at least four have been exceeded. 30x30 initiative commits nations to protect 30% of land and ocean by 2030. Climate change and biodiversity loss are interconnected crises requiring integrated solutions; intact ecosystems are critical carbon sinks and adaptation assets.
`

function parseEnvScience(raw: string): UnitOverview[] {
  const lines = raw.split('\n')
  const units: UnitOverview[] = []

  let currentUnit: UnitOverview | null = null
  let currentSubunit:
    | { id: string; title: string; summaryLines: string[]; keyIdeasLine: string }
    | null = null

  const flushSubunit = () => {
    if (!currentUnit || !currentSubunit) return
    const summary = currentSubunit.summaryLines.join('\n').trim()
    const keyIdeas = currentSubunit.keyIdeasLine ? [currentSubunit.keyIdeasLine] : []

    const sub: SubunitOverview = {
      id: currentSubunit.id,
      title: currentSubunit.title,
      summary,
      keyIdeas,
    }
    currentUnit.subunits.push(sub)
    currentSubunit = null
  }

  const flushUnit = () => {
    if (!currentUnit) return
    units.push(currentUnit)
    currentUnit = null
  }

  for (const line of lines) {
    const unitMatch = line.match(/^Unit (\d+)\s+[–-]\s+(.+)$/)
    if (unitMatch) {
      flushSubunit()
      flushUnit()
      currentUnit = {
        unitNumber: Number(unitMatch[1]),
        title: unitMatch[2].trim(),
        subunits: [],
      }
      continue
    }

    const subunitMatch = line.match(/^(\d+)\.(\d+)\s+(.+)$/)
    if (subunitMatch) {
      flushSubunit()
      if (!currentUnit) continue
      const id = `${subunitMatch[1]}-${subunitMatch[2]}`
      currentSubunit = {
        id,
        title: subunitMatch[3].trim(),
        summaryLines: [],
        keyIdeasLine: '',
      }
      continue
    }

    const keyIdeasMatch = line.match(/^\s*Key ideas:\s*(.+)$/)
    if (keyIdeasMatch && currentSubunit) {
      currentSubunit.keyIdeasLine = keyIdeasMatch[1].trim()
      continue
    }

    if (currentSubunit) currentSubunit.summaryLines.push(line)
  }

  flushSubunit()
  flushUnit()
  return units
}

export const AP_ENV_SCIENCE_UNIT_OVERVIEWS: SubjectUnitOverview = {
  subjectName: 'AP Environmental Science',
  units: parseEnvScience(RAW_AP_ENV_SCIENCE),
}

